---
title: Mutations
id: mutations
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack DB provides a powerful mutation system that enables optimistic updates with automatic state management. This system is built around a pattern of "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"optimistic mutation → mutation handler → handler-defined settlement"}]},{"type":"text","value":". A handler can extend that boundary through backend confirmation by waiting for the write to sync back before it returns. This creates a highly responsive user experience while maintaining data consistency and being easy to reason about."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local changes are applied immediately as optimistic state. When the mutation handler returns successfully while the transaction is still "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persisting"},{"type":"text","value":", the transaction completes and the optimistic state is recomputed. Completion proves backend confirmation only when the handler explicitly waited for that confirmation or read-back."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./offline-transactions.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"offline transactions"}]},{"type":"text","value":" when a mutation must remain pending across an app restart. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./sqlite-persistence.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SQLite persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to retain Collection rows. It can also save sync metadata for supported adapters."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Define a collection with a mutation handler\nconst todoCollection = createCollection({\n  id: \"todos\",\n  onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    const mutation = transaction.mutations[0]\n    await api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n  },\n})\n\n// Apply an optimistic update\ntodoCollection.update(todo.id, (draft) => {\n  draft.completed = true\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This pattern extends the Redux/Flux unidirectional data flow beyond the client to include the server:"}]},{"type":"html","value":"<figure>\n  <a href=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/db/main/docs/unidirectional-data-flow.lg.png\" target=\"_blank\">\n    <img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/db/main/docs/unidirectional-data-flow.png\" />\n  </a>\n</figure>"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The instant inner loop provides optimistic state. The slower outer loop is owned\nby the mutation handler, which can persist to a server and wait for the updated\nserver state to sync back into the collection when that confirmation is\nrequired."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"simplified-mutations-vs-traditional-approaches","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Simplified Mutations vs Traditional Approaches"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack DB's mutation system eliminates much of the boilerplate required for optimistic updates in traditional approaches. Compare the difference:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before (TanStack Query with manual optimistic updates):"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"const addTodoMutation = useMutation({\n  mutationFn: async (newTodo) => api.todos.create(newTodo),\n  onMutate: async (newTodo) => {\n    await queryClient.cancelQueries({ queryKey: ['todos'] })\n    const previousTodos = queryClient.getQueryData(['todos'])\n    queryClient.setQueryData(['todos'], (old) => [...(old || []), newTodo])\n    return { previousTodos }\n  },\n  onError: (err, newTodo, context) => {\n    queryClient.setQueryData(['todos'], context.previousTodos)\n  },\n  onSettled: () => {\n    queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['todos'] })\n  },\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After (TanStack DB):"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"const todoCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: ['todos'],\n    queryFn: async () => api.todos.getAll(),\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    schema: todoSchema,\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await Promise.all(\n        transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n          api.todos.create(mutation.modified)\n        )\n      )\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n// Simple mutation - no boilerplate!\ntodoCollection.insert({\n  id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n  text: '🔥 Make app faster',\n  completed: false,\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The benefits:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅ Automatic optimistic updates"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅ Automatic rollback on error"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅ No manual cache manipulation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅ Type-safe mutations"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"table-of-contents","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table of Contents"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"#mutation-approaches","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutation Approaches"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"#mutation-lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutation Lifecycle"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"#collection-write-operations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection Write Operations"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"#operation-handlers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Operation Handlers"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"#creating-custom-actions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creating Custom Actions"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"#manual-transactions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual Transactions"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"#paced-mutations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Paced Mutations"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"#mutation-merging","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutation Merging"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"#controlling-optimistic-behavior","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlling Optimistic Behavior"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"#transaction-states","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transaction States"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"#handling-temporary-ids","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handling Temporary IDs"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"mutation-approaches","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutation Approaches"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack DB provides different approaches to mutations, each suited to different use cases:"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"collection-level-mutations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection-Level Mutations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection-level mutations ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"insert"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delete"},{"type":"text","value":") are designed for "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"direct state manipulation"}]},{"type":"text","value":" of a single collection. These are the simplest way to make changes and work well for straightforward CRUD operations."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Direct state change\ntodoCollection.update(todoId, (draft) => {\n  draft.completed = true\n  draft.completedAt = new Date()\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use collection-level mutations when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You're making simple CRUD operations on a single collection"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The state changes are straightforward and match what the server will store"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata"},{"type":"text","value":" to annotate these operations and customize behavior in your handlers:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Annotate with metadata\ntodoCollection.update(\n  todoId,\n  { metadata: { intent: 'complete' } },\n  (draft) => {\n    draft.completed = true\n  }\n)\n\n// Use metadata in handler\nonUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n  const mutation = transaction.mutations[0]\n\n  if (mutation.metadata?.intent === 'complete') {\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.todos.complete(mutation.original.id)\n      )\n    )\n  } else {\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n      )\n    )\n  }\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"intent-based-mutations-with-custom-actions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Intent-Based Mutations with Custom Actions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For more complex scenarios, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createOptimisticAction"},{"type":"text","value":" to create "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"intent-based mutations"}]},{"type":"text","value":" that capture specific user actions."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Intent: \"like this post\"\nconst likePost = createOptimisticAction<string>({\n  onMutate: (postId) => {\n    // Optimistic guess at the change\n    postCollection.update(postId, (draft) => {\n      draft.likeCount += 1\n      draft.likedByMe = true\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async (postId) => {\n    // Send the intent to the server\n    await api.posts.like(postId)\n    // Server determines actual state changes\n    await postCollection.utils.refetch()\n  },\n})\n\n// Use it.\nlikePost(postId)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use custom actions when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You need to mutate "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"multiple collections"}]},{"type":"text","value":" in a single transaction"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The optimistic change is a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"guess"}]},{"type":"text","value":" at how the server will transform the data"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want to send "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"user intent"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to the backend rather than exact state changes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The server performs complex logic, calculations, or side effects"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want a clean, reusable mutation that captures a specific operation"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom actions provide the cleanest way to capture specific types of mutations as named operations in your application. While you can achieve similar results using metadata with collection-level mutations, custom actions make the intent explicit and keep related logic together."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Teams that require writes to use actions can "},{"type":"link","href":"#enforcing-action-boundaries-with-eslint","children":[{"type":"text","value":"enforce action boundaries with ESLint"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to use each:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection-level mutations"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection.update"},{"type":"text","value":"): Simple CRUD operations on a single collection"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createOptimisticAction"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Intent-based operations, multi-collection mutations, immediately committed"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bypass the mutation system"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Use your existing mutation logic without rewriting"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"bypass-the-mutation-system","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bypass the Mutation System"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you already have mutation logic in an existing system and don't want to rewrite it, you can "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"completely bypass"}]},{"type":"text","value":" TanStack DB's mutation system and use your existing patterns."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With this approach, you write to the server like normal using your existing logic, then use your collection's mechanism for refetching or syncing data to await the server write. After the sync completes, the collection will have the updated server data and you can render the new state, hide loading indicators, show success messages, navigate to a new page, etc."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Call your backend directly with your existing logic\nconst handleUpdateTodo = async (todoId, changes) => {\n  await api.todos.update(todoId, changes)\n\n  // Wait for the server change to load into the collection\n  await todoCollection.utils.refetch()\n\n  // Now you know the new data is loaded and can render it or hide loaders\n}\n\n// With Electric\nconst handleUpdateTodo = async (todoId, changes) => {\n  const { txid } = await api.todos.update(todoId, changes)\n\n  // Wait for this specific transaction to sync into the collection\n  await todoCollection.utils.awaitTxId(txid)\n\n  // Now the server change is loaded and you can update UI accordingly\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use this approach when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You have existing mutation logic you don't want to rewrite"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You're comfortable with your current mutation patterns"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want to use TanStack DB only for queries and state management"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How to sync changes back:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"QueryCollection"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Refetch with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection.utils.refetch()"},{"type":"text","value":" to reload data from the server"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ElectricCollection"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection.utils.awaitTxId(txid)"},{"type":"text","value":" to wait for a specific transaction to sync"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Other sync systems"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Wait for your sync mechanism to update the collection"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"mutation-lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutation Lifecycle"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The mutation lifecycle follows a consistent pattern across all mutation types:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optimistic state applied"}]},{"type":"text","value":": The mutation is immediately applied to the local collection as optimistic state"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handler invoked"}]},{"type":"text","value":": The appropriate handler — either "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutationFn"},{"type":"text","value":" or a Collection handler ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onInsert"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUpdate"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onDelete"},{"type":"text","value":") — is called to persist the change"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handler-defined persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Your handler performs the required local or backend work"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optional confirmation"}]},{"type":"text","value":": If transaction completion must mean server confirmation, the handler waits for the provider's acknowledgement, read-back, or sync observation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Successful transaction settlement"}]},{"type":"text","value":": If the handler returns while the transaction is still "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persisting"},{"type":"text","value":", the transaction becomes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"completed"},{"type":"text","value":" and the visible state is recomputed from synced data and any remaining optimistic transactions"}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Step 1: Optimistic state applied immediately\ntodoCollection.update(todo.id, (draft) => {\n  draft.completed = true\n})\n// UI updates instantly with optimistic state\n\n// Step 2-3: onUpdate handler performs its persistence work\n// Step 4: The handler may wait for sync back when confirmation is required\n// Step 5: Handler return settles the transaction and recomputes visible state","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the handler throws an error during persistence, the optimistic state is automatically rolled back."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tx.when('settled')"},{"type":"text","value":" observes this transaction-settlement boundary. It resolves\nwith the transaction on success and rejects with the original error on failure\n(or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" for a rollback without an error). It does not by itself prove\nthat a server uploaded, confirmed, or returned the write. It proves those\nstronger guarantees only when the handler waits for that backend observation\nbefore returning. The old "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tx.isPersisted.promise"},{"type":"text","value":" remains available for the\npre-RC releases but is deprecated and will be removed in the 1.0 RC; replace\neach use with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tx.when('settled')"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"concurrent-optimistic-transactions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Concurrent Optimistic Transactions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separate transactions that update the same row are layered as whole-row\nsnapshots. The latest contributing whole-row snapshot supplies the visible row;\nfields are not independently merged from whichever transactions remain\npending. Each later "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update()"},{"type":"text","value":" draft starts from the current visible state,\nincluding optimistic changes from earlier transactions, so edits can build on\none another."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const tx1 = docCollection.update(docId, (draft) => {\n  draft.content = 'A'\n})\nconst tx2 = docCollection.update(docId, (draft) => {\n  // This draft sees content = 'A'.\n  draft.content = 'B'\n})\nconst tx3 = docCollection.update(docId, (draft) => {\n  // This draft sees content = 'B'.\n  draft.content = 'C'\n})\n\n// Visible content is now 'C'. If tx1 and then tx2 settle while tx3 is still\n// active, 'C' remains visible.","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If a newer transaction stops contributing while an older one is still active,\nthe older transaction's snapshot can become visible again until that older\ntransaction itself settles or rolls back. Do not infer server execution order\nfrom the local layering order. Multiple writes to the same row inside one\nmanual transaction are different: they merge into that transaction's single\npending mutation according to the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"#mutation-merging","children":[{"type":"text","value":"mutation-merging rules"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"collection-write-operations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection Write Operations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collections support three core write operations: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"insert"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delete"},{"type":"text","value":". Each operation applies optimistic state immediately and triggers the corresponding operation handler."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"insert","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Insert"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add new items to a collection:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Insert a single item\ntodoCollection.insert({\n  id: \"1\",\n  text: \"Buy groceries\",\n  completed: false\n})\n\n// Insert multiple items\ntodoCollection.insert([\n  { id: \"1\", text: \"Buy groceries\", completed: false },\n  { id: \"2\", text: \"Walk dog\", completed: false },\n])\n\n// Insert with metadata\ntodoCollection.insert(\n  { id: \"1\", text: \"Custom item\", completed: false },\n  { metadata: { source: \"import\" } }\n)\n\n// Insert without optimistic updates\ntodoCollection.insert(\n  { id: \"1\", text: \"Server-validated item\", completed: false },\n  { optimistic: false }\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Returns"}]},{"type":"text","value":": A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Transaction"},{"type":"text","value":" object that you can use to track the mutation's lifecycle."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"update","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Update"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Modify existing items using an immutable draft pattern:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Update a single item\ntodoCollection.update(todo.id, (draft) => {\n  draft.completed = true\n})\n\n// Update multiple items\ntodoCollection.update([todo1.id, todo2.id], (drafts) => {\n  drafts.forEach((draft) => {\n    draft.completed = true\n  })\n})\n\n// Update with metadata\ntodoCollection.update(\n  todo.id,\n  { metadata: { reason: \"user update\" } },\n  (draft) => {\n    draft.text = \"Updated text\"\n  }\n)\n\n// Update without optimistic updates\ntodoCollection.update(\n  todo.id,\n  { optimistic: false },\n  (draft) => {\n    draft.status = \"server-validated\"\n  }\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Parameters"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keys"},{"type":"text","value":": The item key(s) to update"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"options"},{"type":"text","value":" (optional): Configuration object with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata"},{"type":"text","value":" and/or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"optimistic"},{"type":"text","value":" flags"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"updater"},{"type":"text","value":": Function that receives a draft to mutate"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Returns"}]},{"type":"text","value":": A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Transaction"},{"type":"text","value":" object that you can use to track the mutation's lifecycle."}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"important","title":"Important","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"updater"},{"type":"text","value":" function uses an Immer-like pattern to capture changes as immutable updates. You must not reassign the draft parameter itself—only mutate its properties."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Existing row values stay isolated from draft edits. New objects you assign or\nadd to a draft keep normal shared references during the synchronous callback:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const tag = { label: 'new' }\ntodoCollection.update(todoId, (draft) => {\n  draft.tags.add(tag) // tags is a Set\n  tag.label = 'edited' // included in the update\n  for (const value of draft.tags) value.label = 'final'\n  // tag.label is now 'final' too\n})\ntag.label = 'later' // does not change the stored row","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The completed changes are copied when the callback returns. This applies to\nnew Map values, Set members, and objects assigned to draft properties. If you\nneed to keep a new caller-owned object unchanged during the callback, insert\nyour own copy. A thrown callback does not roll back edits to that caller-owned\nobject; it leaves existing collection data unchanged."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Arbitrary class instances are an exception: newly assigned instances stay by\nreference so their methods, prototypes, and private fields remain intact.\nLater changes to such an instance can therefore affect stored data without a\nnew update or notification. Treat those instances as immutable, or convert them\nto plain data before assignment when you need isolation. Supported native values\nsuch as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"URL"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Date"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RegExp"},{"type":"text","value":", ArrayBuffer, DataView, and typed arrays are copied instead. Standard binary views retain their byte range and backing bytes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"delete","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Delete"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Remove items from a collection:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Delete a single item\ntodoCollection.delete(todo.id)\n\n// Delete multiple items\ntodoCollection.delete([todo1.id, todo2.id])\n\n// Delete with metadata\ntodoCollection.delete(todo.id, {\n  metadata: { reason: \"completed\" }\n})\n\n// Delete without optimistic updates\ntodoCollection.delete(todo.id, { optimistic: false })","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Parameters"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keys"},{"type":"text","value":": The item key(s) to delete"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"options"},{"type":"text","value":" (optional): Configuration object with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata"},{"type":"text","value":" and/or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"optimistic"},{"type":"text","value":" flags"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Returns"}]},{"type":"text","value":": A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Transaction"},{"type":"text","value":" object that you can use to track the mutation's lifecycle."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"operation-handlers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Operation Handlers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Operation handlers are functions you provide when creating a collection that handle persisting mutations to your backend. Each collection can define three optional handlers: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onInsert"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUpdate"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onDelete"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"handler-signature","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handler Signature"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All operation handlers receive an object with the following properties:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type OperationHandler = (params: {\n  transaction: Transaction\n  collection: Collection\n}) => Promise<any> | any","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transaction"},{"type":"text","value":" object contains:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutations"},{"type":"text","value":": Array of mutation objects, each with:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection"},{"type":"text","value":": The collection being mutated"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"type"},{"type":"text","value":": The mutation type ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'insert'"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'update'"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'delete'"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"original"},{"type":"text","value":": The original item (for updates and deletes)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"modified"},{"type":"text","value":": The modified item (for inserts and updates)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"changes"},{"type":"text","value":": The changes object (for updates)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":": The item key"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata"},{"type":"text","value":": Optional metadata attached to the mutation"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"defining-operation-handlers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Defining Operation Handlers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Define handlers when creating a collection:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const todoCollection = createCollection({\n  id: \"todos\",\n  // ... other options\n\n  onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.todos.create(mutation.modified)\n      )\n    )\n  },\n\n  onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n      )\n    )\n  },\n\n  onDelete: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.todos.delete(mutation.original.id)\n      )\n    )\n  },\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"callout","kind":"important","title":"Important","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Operation handlers must not resolve until the server changes have synced back to the collection. Different collection types provide different patterns to ensure this happens correctly."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not call or await "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection.preload()"},{"type":"text","value":", live-query "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preload()"},{"type":"text","value":", or a\ndirect "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadSubset()"},{"type":"text","value":" inside a mutation handler. The optimistic mutation is\nalready applied when the handler starts. A preload may need a sync commit\nthat is queued behind that same handler, which creates a deadlock. Use the\ncollection adapter's documented mutation acknowledgement pattern instead."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"collection-specific-handler-patterns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection-Specific Handler Patterns"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Different collection types have specific patterns for their handlers:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"QueryCollection"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - currently refetches automatically after persisting changes. To adopt the explicit v1.0 pattern now:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"onUpdate: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n  await Promise.all(\n    transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n      api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n    )\n  )\n  // Trigger refetch to sync server state\n  await collection.utils.refetch()\n  // Prevent the current pre-1.0 wrapper from refetching a second time.\n  // Remove this return in v1.0.\n  return { refetch: false }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ElectricCollection"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - wait for txid(s) to sync:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"onUpdate: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n  await Promise.all(\n    transaction.mutations.map(async (mutation) => {\n      const response = await api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n      // Wait for this txid to sync\n      await collection.utils.awaitTxId(response.txid)\n    })\n  )\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"generic-mutation-functions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generic Mutation Functions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can define a single mutation function for your entire app:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { MutationFn } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nconst mutationFn: MutationFn = async ({ transaction }) => {\n  const response = await api.mutations.batch(transaction.mutations)\n\n  if (!response.ok) {\n    throw new Error(`HTTP Error: ${response.status}`)\n  }\n}\n\n// Use in collections\nconst todoCollection = createCollection({\n  id: \"todos\",\n  onInsert: mutationFn,\n  onUpdate: mutationFn,\n  onDelete: mutationFn,\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"schema-validation-in-mutation-handlers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schema Validation in Mutation Handlers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a schema is configured for a collection, TanStack DB automatically validates and transforms data during mutations. The mutation handlers receive the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"transformed data"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (TOutput), not the raw input."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const todoSchema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  text: z.string(),\n  created_at: z.string().transform(val => new Date(val))  // TInput: string, TOutput: Date\n})\n\nconst collection = createCollection({\n  schema: todoSchema,\n  onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    const item = transaction.mutations[0].modified\n\n    // item.created_at is already a Date object (TOutput)\n    console.log(item.created_at instanceof Date)  // true\n\n    // If your API needs a string, serialize it\n    await api.todos.create({\n      ...item,\n      created_at: item.created_at.toISOString()  // Date → string\n    })\n  }\n})\n\n// User provides string (TInput)\ncollection.insert({\n  id: \"1\",\n  text: \"Task\",\n  created_at: \"2024-01-01T00:00:00Z\"\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key points:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schema validation happens "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"before"}]},{"type":"text","value":" mutation handlers are called"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handlers receive "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TOutput"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (transformed data)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your backend needs a different format, serialize in the handler"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schema validation errors throw "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SchemaValidationError"},{"type":"text","value":" before handlers run"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For comprehensive documentation on schema validation and transformations, see the "},{"type":"link","href":"./schemas.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schemas guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"creating-custom-actions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creating Custom Actions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For more complex mutation patterns, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createOptimisticAction"},{"type":"text","value":" to create custom actions with full control over the mutation lifecycle."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"basic-action","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic Action"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create an action that combines mutation logic with persistence:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createOptimisticAction } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nconst addTodo = createOptimisticAction<string>({\n  onMutate: (text) => {\n    // Apply optimistic state\n    todoCollection.insert({\n      id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n      text,\n      completed: false,\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async (text, params) => {\n    // Persist to backend\n    const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\", {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      body: JSON.stringify({ text, completed: false }),\n    })\n    const result = await response.json()\n\n    // Wait for sync back\n    await todoCollection.utils.refetch()\n\n    return result\n  },\n})\n\n// Use in components\nconst Todo = () => {\n  const handleClick = () => {\n    addTodo(\"🔥 Make app faster\")\n  }\n\n  return <Button onClick={handleClick} />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"type-safe-actions-with-schema-validation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type-Safe Actions with Schema Validation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For better type safety and runtime validation, you can use schema validation libraries like Zod, Valibot, or others. Here's an example using Zod:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createOptimisticAction } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { z } from \"zod\"\n\n// Define a schema for the action parameters\nconst addTodoSchema = z.object({\n  text: z.string().min(1, \"Todo text cannot be empty\"),\n  priority: z.enum([\"low\", \"medium\", \"high\"]).optional(),\n})\n\n// Use the schema's inferred type for the generic\nconst addTodo = createOptimisticAction<z.infer<typeof addTodoSchema>>({\n  onMutate: (params) => {\n    // Validate parameters at runtime\n    const validated = addTodoSchema.parse(params)\n\n    // Apply optimistic state\n    todoCollection.insert({\n      id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n      text: validated.text,\n      priority: validated.priority ?? \"medium\",\n      completed: false,\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async (params) => {\n    // Parameters are already validated\n    const validated = addTodoSchema.parse(params)\n\n    const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\", {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      body: JSON.stringify({\n        text: validated.text,\n        priority: validated.priority ?? \"medium\",\n        completed: false,\n      }),\n    })\n    const result = await response.json()\n\n    await todoCollection.utils.refetch()\n    return result\n  },\n})\n\n// Use with type-safe parameters\nconst Todo = () => {\n  const handleClick = () => {\n    addTodo({\n      text: \"🔥 Make app faster\",\n      priority: \"high\",\n    })\n  }\n\n  return <Button onClick={handleClick} />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This pattern works with any validation library (Zod, Valibot, Yup, etc.) and provides:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅ Runtime validation of parameters"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅ Type safety from inferred types"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅ Clear error messages for invalid inputs"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅ Single source of truth for parameter shape"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"complex-multi-collection-actions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Complex Multi-Collection Actions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Actions can mutate multiple collections:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const createProject = createOptimisticAction<{\n  name: string\n  ownerId: string\n}>({\n  onMutate: ({ name, ownerId }) => {\n    const projectId = crypto.randomUUID()\n\n    // Insert project\n    projectCollection.insert({\n      id: projectId,\n      name,\n      ownerId,\n      createdAt: new Date(),\n    })\n\n    // Update user's project count\n    userCollection.update(ownerId, (draft) => {\n      draft.projectCount += 1\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async ({ name, ownerId }) => {\n    const response = await api.projects.create({ name, ownerId })\n\n    // Wait for both collections to sync\n    await Promise.all([\n      projectCollection.utils.refetch(),\n      userCollection.utils.refetch(),\n    ])\n\n    return response\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"action-parameters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Action Parameters"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutationFn"},{"type":"text","value":" receives additional parameters for advanced use cases:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const updateTodo = createOptimisticAction<{\n  id: string\n  changes: Partial<Todo>\n}>({\n  onMutate: ({ id, changes }) => {\n    todoCollection.update(id, (draft) => {\n      Object.assign(draft, changes)\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async ({ id, changes }, params) => {\n    // params.transaction contains the transaction object\n    // params.signal is an AbortSignal for cancellation\n\n    const response = await api.todos.update(id, changes, {\n      signal: params.signal,\n    })\n\n    await todoCollection.utils.refetch()\n    return response\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"enforcing-action-boundaries-with-eslint","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enforcing Action Boundaries with ESLint"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Some teams require UI writes to use actions so shared validation, side effects, and persistence logic remain together. This is an optional application convention. Direct Collection mutations remain appropriate for simple CRUD operations."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/examples/react/action-enforcement","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React action-enforcement example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" shows two ESLint policies. Its custom rule permits direct Collection reads in feature code and rejects calls to configured mutation methods on matching Collection imports. The stricter "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"no-restricted-imports"},{"type":"text","value":" alternative prohibits those imports, so feature code uses query hooks for reads and actions for writes. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/blob/main/examples/react/action-enforcement/README.md#what-is-enforced","children":[{"type":"text","value":"example README"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for setup, configurable import paths, and the limits of its static analysis."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"manual-transactions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual Transactions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For maximum control over transaction lifecycles, create transactions manually using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTransaction"},{"type":"text","value":". This approach allows you to batch multiple mutations, implement custom commit workflows, or create transactions that span multiple user interactions."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"basic-manual-transaction","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic Manual Transaction"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createTransaction } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nconst addTodoTx = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    // Persist all mutations to backend\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.saveTodo(mutation.modified)\n      )\n    )\n  },\n})\n\n// Apply first change\naddTodoTx.mutate(() =>\n  todoCollection.insert({\n    id: \"1\",\n    text: \"First todo\",\n    completed: false\n  })\n)\n\n// User reviews change...\n\n// Apply another change\naddTodoTx.mutate(() =>\n  todoCollection.insert({\n    id: \"2\",\n    text: \"Second todo\",\n    completed: false\n  })\n)\n\n// User commits when ready (e.g., when they hit save)\naddTodoTx.commit()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"transaction-configuration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transaction Configuration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual transactions accept the following options:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createTransaction({\n  id?: string,              // Optional unique identifier for the transaction\n  autoCommit?: boolean,     // Whether to automatically commit after mutate()\n  mutationFn: MutationFn,   // Function to persist mutations\n  metadata?: Record<string, unknown>, // Optional custom metadata\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"autoCommit"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" (default): Transaction commits immediately after each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutate()"},{"type":"text","value":" call"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":": Transaction waits for explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit()"},{"type":"text","value":" call"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"transaction-methods","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transaction Methods"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual transactions provide several methods:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Apply mutations within a transaction\ntx.mutate(() => {\n  collection.insert(item)\n  collection.update(key, updater)\n})\n\n// Commit the transaction\nawait tx.commit()\n\n// Manually rollback changes (e.g., user cancels a form)\n// Note: Rollback happens automatically if mutationFn throws an error\ntx.rollback()","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you call collection methods inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tx.mutate()"},{"type":"text","value":", the mutations are\ncaptured by the manual transaction. The collection's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onInsert"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUpdate"},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onDelete"},{"type":"text","value":" handlers are not invoked for those mutations; the\nmanual transaction's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutationFn"},{"type":"text","value":" is responsible for persisting\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transaction.mutations"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tx.mutate()"},{"type":"text","value":" callback throws, that call's optimistic changes are removed\nbefore the error reaches the caller. Earlier successful "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tx.mutate()"},{"type":"text","value":" calls\nremain in the pending transaction. Collection operations can notify subscribers\nwhile the callback runs, so a subscriber may see a change followed by its\nrestoration when a later operation throws.\nIf a subscriber also throws during restoration, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutate()"},{"type":"text","value":" throws an\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AggregateError"},{"type":"text","value":" whose cause is the original callback error; it still restores\nevery touched Collection."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This makes manual transactions a good fit for draft-style workflows where local\nstate should update immediately, but persistence should wait for a later user\naction such as Save or Blur. Each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tx.mutate()"},{"type":"text","value":" call updates the optimistic\nstate instantly, so the UI reflects changes as the user types. When the user\ntriggers Save, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tx.commit()"},{"type":"text","value":" fires the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutationFn"},{"type":"text","value":" to persist all accumulated\nmutations in a single batch. If the user cancels instead, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tx.rollback()"},{"type":"text","value":"\ndiscards the optimistic changes and reverts the UI."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"multi-step-workflows","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-Step Workflows"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual transactions excel at complex workflows:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const reviewTx = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await api.batchUpdate(transaction.mutations)\n  },\n})\n\n// Step 1: User makes initial changes\nreviewTx.mutate(() => {\n  todoCollection.update(id1, (draft) => {\n    draft.status = \"reviewed\"\n  })\n  todoCollection.update(id2, (draft) => {\n    draft.status = \"reviewed\"\n  })\n})\n\n// Step 2: Show preview to user...\n\n// Step 3: User confirms or makes additional changes\nreviewTx.mutate(() => {\n  todoCollection.update(id3, (draft) => {\n    draft.status = \"reviewed\"\n  })\n})\n\n// Step 4: User commits all changes at once\nawait reviewTx.commit()\n// OR user cancels\n// reviewTx.rollback()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"using-with-local-collections","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using with Local Collections"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"LocalOnly and LocalStorage collections require special handling when used with manual transactions. Unlike server-synced collections that have "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onInsert"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUpdate"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onDelete"},{"type":"text","value":" handlers automatically invoked, local collections need you to manually accept mutations by calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"utils.acceptMutations()"},{"type":"text","value":" in your transaction's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutationFn"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"why-this-is-needed","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why This Is Needed"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local collections (LocalOnly and LocalStorage) don't participate in the standard mutation handler flow for manual transactions. They need an explicit call to persist changes made during "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tx.mutate()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"basic-usage","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic Usage"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createTransaction } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { localOnlyCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nconst formDraft = createCollection(\n  localOnlyCollectionOptions({\n    id: \"form-draft\",\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  })\n)\n\nconst tx = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    // Make API call with the data first\n    const draftData = transaction.mutations\n      .filter((m) => m.collection === formDraft)\n      .map((m) => m.modified)\n\n    await api.saveDraft(draftData)\n\n    // After API succeeds, accept and persist local collection mutations\n    await formDraft.utils.acceptMutations(transaction)\n  },\n})\n\n// Apply mutations\ntx.mutate(() => {\n  formDraft.insert({ id: \"1\", field: \"value\" })\n})\n\n// Commit when ready\nawait tx.commit()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"combining-local-and-server-collections","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Combining Local and Server Collections"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can mix local and server collections in the same transaction:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const localSettings = createCollection(\n  localStorageCollectionOptions({\n    id: \"user-settings\",\n    storageKey: \"app-settings\",\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  })\n)\n\nconst userProfile = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"profile\"],\n    queryFn: async () => api.profile.get(),\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await api.profile.update(transaction.mutations[0].modified)\n    },\n  })\n)\n\nconst tx = createTransaction({\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    // Handle server collection mutations explicitly in mutationFn\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations\n        .filter((m) => m.collection === userProfile)\n        .map((m) => api.profile.update(m.modified))\n    )\n\n    // After server mutations succeed, accept local collection mutations\n    await localSettings.utils.acceptMutations(transaction)\n  },\n})\n\n// Update both local and server data in one transaction\ntx.mutate(() => {\n  localSettings.update(\"theme\", (draft) => {\n    draft.mode = \"dark\"\n  })\n  userProfile.update(\"user-1\", (draft) => {\n    draft.name = \"Updated Name\"\n  })\n})\n\nawait tx.commit()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"transaction-ordering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transaction Ordering"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"acceptMutations"}]},{"type":"text","value":" matters for transaction semantics:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After API success (recommended for consistency):"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n  await api.save(data)  // API call first\n  await localData.utils.acceptMutations(transaction)  // Persist after success\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅ "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pros"}]},{"type":"text","value":": If the API fails, local changes roll back too (all-or-nothing semantics)\n❌ "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cons"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Local state won't reflect changes until API succeeds"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before API call (for independent local state):"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n  await localData.utils.acceptMutations(transaction)  // Persist first\n  await api.save(data)  // Then API call\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"✅ "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pros"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Local state persists immediately, regardless of API outcome\n❌ "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cons"}]},{"type":"text","value":": API failure leaves local changes persisted (divergent state)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose based on whether your local data should be independent of or coupled to remote mutations."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"best-practices","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Best Practices"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Always call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"utils.acceptMutations()"},{"type":"text","value":" for local collections in manual transactions. LocalStorage transaction settlement waits for its storage write even if the returned Promise is not awaited; await it when later work depends on the write"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"acceptMutations"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"after"}]},{"type":"text","value":" API success if you want transactional consistency"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"acceptMutations"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"before"}]},{"type":"text","value":" API calls if local state should persist regardless"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Filter mutations by collection if you need to process them separately"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mix local and server collections freely in the same transaction"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"listening-to-transaction-lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Listening to Transaction Lifecycle"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Monitor transaction state changes:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const tx = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await api.persist(transaction.mutations)\n  },\n})\n\n// Wait for the transaction handler to settle\ntx.when('settled').then(() => {\n  console.log(\"Transaction completed!\")\n})\n\n// Check current state\nconsole.log(tx.state) // 'pending', 'persisting', 'completed', or 'failed'","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"paced-mutations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Paced Mutations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Paced mutations provide fine-grained control over "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"when and how"}]},{"type":"text","value":" mutations are persisted to your backend. Instead of persisting every mutation immediately, you can use timing strategies to batch, delay, or queue mutations based on your application's needs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Powered by "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/pacer","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Pacer"}]},{"type":"text","value":", paced mutations are ideal for scenarios like:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auto-save forms"}]},{"type":"text","value":" that wait for the user to stop typing"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Slider controls"}]},{"type":"text","value":" that need smooth updates without overwhelming the backend"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequential workflows"}]},{"type":"text","value":" where admitted mutations must be attempted in order"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"key-design","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key Design"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The fundamental difference between strategies is how they handle transactions:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Debounce/Throttle"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Only one pending transaction (collecting mutations) and one persisting transaction (writing to backend) at a time. Multiple rapid mutations automatically merge together into a single transaction."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Queue"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Each mutation creates a separate transaction. Admitted mutations run in queue order (FIFO by default, configurable to LIFO). If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxSize"},{"type":"text","value":" is set and the waiting queue is full, the returned transaction fails and its optimistic state rolls back."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"available-strategies","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Available Strategies"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Strategy"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Behavior"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Best For"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"debounceStrategy"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wait for inactivity before persisting. Only final state is saved."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auto-save forms, search-as-you-type"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throttleStrategy"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ensure minimum spacing between executions. Mutations between executions are merged."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sliders, progress updates, analytics"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queueStrategy"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each mutation becomes a separate transaction. Admitted mutations are attempted sequentially in queue order (FIFO by default, configurable to LIFO)."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequential workflows, file uploads, rate-limited APIs"}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"debounce-strategy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Debounce Strategy"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The debounce strategy waits for a period of inactivity before persisting. This is perfect for auto-save scenarios where you want to wait until the user stops typing before saving their work."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { usePacedMutations, debounceStrategy } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nfunction AutoSaveForm({ formId }: { formId: string }) {\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations<{ field: string; value: string }>({\n    onMutate: ({ field, value }) => {\n      // Apply optimistic update immediately\n      formCollection.update(formId, (draft) => {\n        draft[field] = value\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      // Persist the final merged state to the backend\n      await api.forms.save(transaction.mutations)\n    },\n    // Wait 500ms after the last change before persisting\n    strategy: debounceStrategy({ wait: 500 }),\n  })\n\n  const handleChange = (field: string, value: string) => {\n    // Multiple rapid changes merge into a single transaction\n    mutate({ field, value })\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <form>\n      <input onChange={(e) => handleChange('title', e.target.value)} />\n      <textarea onChange={(e) => handleChange('content', e.target.value)} />\n    </form>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key characteristics"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Timer resets on each mutation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Only the final merged state persists"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reduces backend writes significantly for rapid changes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Omitted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"trailing"},{"type":"text","value":" enables trailing execution, even when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"leading"},{"type":"text","value":" is set"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"trailing: false"},{"type":"text","value":", calls skipped inside the leading window reject with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DebounceCallDroppedError"},{"type":"text","value":" and roll back their optimistic changes. Both edges disabled reject every call."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strategy.cleanup()"},{"type":"text","value":" leaves a pending debounce write scheduled for the quiet edge after its last call. Cleanup returns before the write settles. Await its persistence promise before releasing an external client that the write needs. New debounce calls after cleanup have no defined admission rule."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"throttle-strategy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Throttle Strategy"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The throttle strategy ensures a minimum spacing between executions. This is ideal for scenarios like sliders or progress updates where you want smooth, consistent updates without overwhelming your backend."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { usePacedMutations, throttleStrategy } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nfunction VolumeSlider() {\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations<number>({\n    onMutate: (volume) => {\n      // Apply optimistic update immediately\n      settingsCollection.update('volume', (draft) => {\n        draft.value = volume\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await api.settings.updateVolume(transaction.mutations)\n    },\n    // Persist at most once every 200ms\n    strategy: throttleStrategy({\n      wait: 200,\n      leading: true,   // Execute immediately on first call\n      trailing: true,  // Execute after wait period if there were mutations\n    }),\n  })\n\n  const handleVolumeChange = (volume: number) => {\n    mutate(volume)\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <input\n      type=\"range\"\n      min={0}\n      max={100}\n      onChange={(e) => handleVolumeChange(Number(e.target.value))}\n    />\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key characteristics"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Guarantees minimum spacing between persists"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Can execute on leading edge, trailing edge, or both"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"leading: false, trailing: true"},{"type":"text","value":", the first write waits until the first trailing edge"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"leading: false"},{"type":"text","value":", omitting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"trailing"},{"type":"text","value":" also enables the trailing edge"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"trailing: false"},{"type":"text","value":", calls inside a throttle window fail immediately with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ThrottleCallDroppedError"},{"type":"text","value":" and roll back their optimistic changes. Omitting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"leading"},{"type":"text","value":" in this case enables the leading edge."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With both edges disabled, every call fails with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ThrottleCallDroppedError"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutations between executions are merged"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strategy.cleanup()"},{"type":"text","value":" leaves an already scheduled trailing write at its regular edge, so its optimistic transaction and persistence promise can settle. Cleanup returns before that write settles. Stop invoking the mutation function when its strategy is no longer needed; throttle admission after cleanup is not a defined cancellation boundary."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"queue-strategy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Queue Strategy"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The queue strategy creates a separate transaction for each mutation and processes admitted transactions sequentially in queue order. Unlike debounce/throttle, "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"every admitted mutation is attempted"}]},{"type":"text","value":". When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxSize"},{"type":"text","value":" is set, overflow is rejected at admission rather than silently dropped."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strategy.cleanup()"},{"type":"text","value":" stops new admission. The existing timer drains admitted waiting mutations at the configured "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wait"},{"type":"text","value":" interval and in queue order. Cleanup returns before those writes settle.\nIf the Collection is cleaned up separately, admitted persistence callbacks still run; callers should await the returned transactions when they need those writes to settle before finishing their own teardown."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { usePacedMutations, queueStrategy } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nfunction FileUploader() {\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations<File>({\n    onMutate: (file) => {\n      // Apply optimistic update immediately\n      uploadCollection.insert({\n        id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n        file,\n        status: 'pending',\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      // Each file upload is its own transaction\n      const mutation = transaction.mutations[0]\n      await api.files.upload(mutation.modified)\n    },\n    // Process each upload sequentially with 500ms between them\n    strategy: queueStrategy({\n      wait: 500,\n      addItemsTo: 'back',    // FIFO: add to back of queue\n      getItemsFrom: 'front', // FIFO: process from front of queue\n    }),\n  })\n\n  const handleFileSelect = (files: FileList) => {\n    // Each file creates its own transaction, queued for sequential processing\n    Array.from(files).forEach((file) => {\n      mutate(file)\n    })\n  }\n\n  return <input type=\"file\" multiple onChange={(e) => handleFileSelect(e.target.files!)} />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key characteristics"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each mutation becomes its own transaction"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Processes sequentially in order (FIFO by default)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Can configure to LIFO by setting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getItemsFrom: 'back'"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every admitted mutation is attempted (unlike debounce/throttle which may merge intermediate mutations)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxSize"},{"type":"text","value":" limits waiting items; overflow fails the returned transaction and rolls back its optimistic state. Admission happens before processing, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxSize: 0"},{"type":"text","value":" rejects even the first mutation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Waits for each transaction to complete before starting the next"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Error handling"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If a mutation fails, "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"it is not automatically retried"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - the transaction transitions to \"failed\" state"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Failed mutations surface their error via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transaction.when('settled')"},{"type":"text","value":" (which will reject)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Queue overflow rejects "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transaction.when('settled')"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueueCapacityExceededError"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A call after queue cleanup rejects "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transaction.when('settled')"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueueDisposedError"},{"type":"text","value":" and rolls back its optimistic mutation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Subsequent mutations continue processing"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - a single failure does not block the queue"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each mutation is independent; there is no all-or-nothing transaction semantics across multiple mutations"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To implement retry logic, see "},{"type":"link","href":"#retry-behavior","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Retry Behavior"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"choosing-a-strategy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choosing a Strategy"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use this guide to pick the right strategy for your use case:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"debounceStrategy"},{"type":"text","value":" when:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want to wait for the user to finish their action"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Only the final state matters (intermediate states can be discarded)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want to minimize backend writes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples: auto-save forms, search-as-you-type, settings panels"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throttleStrategy"},{"type":"text","value":" when:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want smooth, consistent updates at a controlled rate"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Some intermediate states should persist, but not all"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You need updates to feel responsive without overwhelming the backend"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples: volume sliders, progress bars, analytics tracking, live cursor position"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queueStrategy"},{"type":"text","value":" when:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every mutation must persist (no operations can be lost)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Order of operations matters"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You're working with a rate-limited API"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You need sequential processing with delays"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples: file uploads, batch operations, audit trails, multi-step wizards"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"using-in-react","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using in React"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"usePacedMutations"},{"type":"text","value":" hook makes it easy to use paced mutations in React components:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { usePacedMutations, debounceStrategy } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nfunction MyComponent({ itemId }: { itemId: string }) {\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations<number>({\n    onMutate: (newValue) => {\n      // Apply optimistic update immediately\n      collection.update(itemId, (draft) => {\n        draft.value = newValue\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await api.save(transaction.mutations)\n    },\n    strategy: debounceStrategy({ wait: 500 }),\n  })\n\n  // Each mutate call returns a Transaction you can await\n  const handleSave = async (newValue: number) => {\n    const tx = mutate(newValue)\n\n    // Optionally wait for handler settlement\n    try {\n      await tx.when('settled')\n      console.log('Transaction completed!')\n    } catch (error) {\n      console.error('Save failed:', error)\n    }\n  }\n\n  return <button onClick={() => handleSave(42)}>Save</button>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hook automatically memoizes the strategy and mutation function to prevent unnecessary recreations. You can also use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createPacedMutations"},{"type":"text","value":" directly outside of React:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createPacedMutations, queueStrategy } from \"@tanstack/db\"\n\nconst mutate = createPacedMutations<{ id: string; changes: Partial<Item> }>({\n  onMutate: ({ id, changes }) => {\n    // Apply optimistic update immediately\n    collection.update(id, (draft) => {\n      Object.assign(draft, changes)\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await api.save(transaction.mutations)\n  },\n  strategy: queueStrategy({ wait: 200 }),\n})\n\n// Use anywhere in your application\nmutate({ id: '123', changes: { name: 'New Name' } })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A paced mutation's returned transaction is a receipt for strategy-owned\npersistence. Await "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tx.when('settled')"},{"type":"text","value":" to observe completion, or call\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tx.rollback()"},{"type":"text","value":" to cancel it. Calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tx.commit()"},{"type":"text","value":" throws immediately because\nit would bypass the strategy's timer and serialization. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTransaction"},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor a workflow that needs manual commit control."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calls admitted into the same pending paced transaction settle together. If an\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onMutate"},{"type":"text","value":" callback calls the paced mutation function again and then throws,\nthe shared transaction rolls back: both the nested call and any earlier call\nmerged into it reject with that error. A later call starts a new transaction."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"understanding-queues-and-hook-instances","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Understanding Queues and Hook Instances"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"usePacedMutations"},{"type":"text","value":" hook call creates its own transaction group. With a\nfresh strategy object, it also has its own timer or queue."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you have multiple components calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"usePacedMutations"},{"type":"text","value":" separately, each will have its own isolated queue:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function EmailDraftEditor1({ draftId }: { draftId: string }) {\n  // This creates Queue A\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations({\n    onMutate: (text) => {\n      draftCollection.update(draftId, (draft) => {\n        draft.text = text\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await api.saveDraft(transaction.mutations)\n    },\n    strategy: debounceStrategy({ wait: 500 }),\n  })\n\n  return <textarea onChange={(e) => mutate(e.target.value)} />\n}\n\nfunction EmailDraftEditor2({ draftId }: { draftId: string }) {\n  // This creates Queue B (separate from Queue A)\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations({\n    onMutate: (text) => {\n      draftCollection.update(draftId, (draft) => {\n        draft.text = text\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await api.saveDraft(transaction.mutations)\n    },\n    strategy: debounceStrategy({ wait: 500 }),\n  })\n\n  return <textarea onChange={(e) => mutate(e.target.value)} />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this example, mutations from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"EmailDraftEditor1"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"EmailDraftEditor2"},{"type":"text","value":" will be queued and processed "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"independently"}]},{"type":"text","value":". They won't share the same debounce timer or queue."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To share the same queue across multiple components"}]},{"type":"text","value":", create a single "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createPacedMutations"},{"type":"text","value":" instance and use it everywhere:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Create a single shared instance\nimport { createPacedMutations, debounceStrategy } from \"@tanstack/db\"\n\nexport const mutateDraft = createPacedMutations<{ draftId: string; text: string }>({\n  onMutate: ({ draftId, text }) => {\n    draftCollection.update(draftId, (draft) => {\n      draft.text = text\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await api.saveDraft(transaction.mutations)\n  },\n  strategy: debounceStrategy({ wait: 500 }),\n})\n\n// Now both components share the same queue\nfunction EmailDraftEditor1({ draftId }: { draftId: string }) {\n  return <textarea onChange={(e) => mutateDraft({ draftId, text: e.target.value })} />\n}\n\nfunction EmailDraftEditor2({ draftId }: { draftId: string }) {\n  return <textarea onChange={(e) => mutateDraft({ draftId, text: e.target.value })} />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With this approach, calls from both components can merge into one pending\ntransaction and share its debounce timer."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two separate managers can instead reuse the same strategy object when they\nneed one timing boundary but separate transactions. A shared debounce timer\nwaits for quiet across both managers, then persists each pending transaction.\nA shared throttle spaces their starts, and a shared queue processes their\ntransactions in queue order. Each returned transaction settles independently."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key takeaways:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One manager shared across components lets calls merge into one pending transaction."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One strategy object shared across managers gives them one timing boundary while retaining separate transactions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separate strategy objects give managers independent timers or queues."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"mutation-merging","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutation Merging"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When multiple mutations operate on the same item within a transaction, TanStack DB intelligently merges them to:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reduce network traffic"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Fewer mutations sent to the server"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserve user intent"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Final state matches what user expects"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Maintain UI consistency"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Local state always reflects user actions"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The merging behavior follows a truth table based on the mutation types:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Existing → New"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Description"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"insert + update"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"insert"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keeps insert type, merges changes, empty original"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"insert + delete"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"removed"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutations cancel each other out"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"update + delete"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delete"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Delete dominates"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"update + update"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Union changes, keep first original"}]}]]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Attempting to insert or delete the same item multiple times within a transaction will throw an error."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"controlling-optimistic-behavior","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlling Optimistic Behavior"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, all mutations apply optimistic updates immediately to provide instant feedback. You can disable this behavior when you do not want a pending transaction to affect the local view. With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"optimistic: false"},{"type":"text","value":", the view changes only when the collection receives new synced or local data; successful handler settlement does not publish the mutation by itself."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"when-to-disable-optimistic-updates","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to Disable Optimistic Updates"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consider using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"optimistic: false"},{"type":"text","value":" when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Complex server-side processing"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Operations that depend on server-side generation (e.g., cascading foreign keys, computed fields)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Validation requirements"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Operations where backend validation might reject the change"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmation workflows"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Deletes where data should remain visible until confirmed synced data removes it"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Batch operations"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Large operations where optimistic rollback would be disruptive"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"behavior-differences","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Behavior Differences"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"optimistic: true"},{"type":"text","value":" (default)"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Immediately applies mutation to the local store"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provides instant UI feedback"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requires rollback if server rejects the mutation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Best for simple, predictable operations"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"optimistic: false"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Does not modify the local view while the handler is pending"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No optimistic view change to roll back"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The view updates only when the collection publishes new synced or local data"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Best for complex or validation-heavy operations"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"using-non-optimistic-mutations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using Non-Optimistic Mutations"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Critical deletion that needs confirmation\nconst handleDeleteAccount = () => {\n  userCollection.delete(userId, { optimistic: false })\n}\n\n// Server-generated data\nconst handleCreateInvoice = () => {\n  // Server generates invoice number, tax calculations, etc.\n  invoiceCollection.insert(invoiceData, { optimistic: false })\n}\n\n// Mixed approach in same transaction\ntx.mutate(() => {\n  // Instant UI feedback for simple change\n  todoCollection.update(todoId, (draft) => {\n    draft.completed = true\n  })\n\n  // The transaction mutationFn must cause the confirmed data to sync.\n  auditCollection.insert(auditRecord, { optimistic: false })\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"waiting-for-handler-settlement","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Waiting for Handler Settlement"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A common pattern with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"optimistic: false"},{"type":"text","value":" is to wait for the mutation handler to\ncomplete before navigating or showing success feedback. If the feedback means\n\"confirmed and published by the server,\" configure the handler to wait for both\nthe confirmation and the relevant sync observation before returning:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const handleCreatePost = async (postData) => {\n  // Insert without optimistic updates\n  const tx = postsCollection.insert(postData, { optimistic: false })\n\n  try {\n    // Wait for this transaction's handler to complete.\n    await tx.when('settled')\n\n    // This is confirmed and published only if the handler awaited both.\n    navigate(`/posts/${postData.id}`)\n  } catch (error) {\n    // Show error notification\n    toast.error(\"Failed to create post: \" + error.message)\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"transaction-states","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transaction States"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transactions progress through the following states during their lifecycle:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pending"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Initial state when a transaction is created and optimistic mutations can be applied"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persisting"}]},{"type":"text","value":": The transaction's mutation handler is running"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"completed"}]},{"type":"text","value":": The mutation handler returned successfully while the transaction was still "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persisting"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"failed"}]},{"type":"text","value":": The transaction was rolled back or its mutation handler threw"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These are local transaction states. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"completed"},{"type":"text","value":" means server-confirmed only if\nthe mutation handler waited for the relevant backend acknowledgement or sync\nobservation before returning."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"monitoring-transaction-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Monitoring Transaction State"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const tx = todoCollection.update(todoId, (draft) => {\n  draft.completed = true\n})\n\n// Check current state\nconsole.log(tx.state) // 'pending'\n\n// Wait for specific states\nawait tx.when('settled')\nconsole.log(tx.state) // 'completed'; a rejection takes the transaction to 'failed'\n\n// Handle errors\ntry {\n  await tx.when('settled')\n  console.log(\"Success!\")\n} catch (error) {\n  console.log(\"Failed:\", error)\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"state-transitions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State Transitions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The normal flow is: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pending"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persisting"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"completed"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If an error occurs: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pending"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persisting"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"failed"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Failed transactions automatically rollback their optimistic state."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"retry-behavior","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Retry Behavior"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Important:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" TanStack DB does not automatically retry failed mutations. If a mutation fails (network error, server error, etc.), the transaction transitions to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"failed"},{"type":"text","value":" state and the optimistic state is rolled back. This is by design. Automatic retry logic varies significantly based on your use case (idempotency requirements, error types, backoff strategies, etc.)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To implement retry logic, wrap your API calls in your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutationFn"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Simple retry helper\nasync function withRetry<T>(\n  fn: () => Promise<T>,\n  maxRetries = 3,\n  delay = 1000\n): Promise<T> {\n  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < maxRetries; attempt++) {\n    try {\n      return await fn()\n    } catch (error) {\n      if (attempt === maxRetries - 1) throw error\n      await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, delay * (attempt + 1)))\n    }\n  }\n  throw new Error('Unreachable')\n}\n\n// Use in your collection\nconst todoCollection = createCollection({\n  id: \"todos\",\n  onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    const mutation = transaction.mutations[0]\n    // Retry up to 3 times with increasing delay\n    await withRetry(() =>\n      api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n    )\n  },\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For more sophisticated retry strategies, consider using a library like "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/sindresorhus/p-retry","children":[{"type":"text","value":"p-retry"}]},{"type":"text","value":" which supports exponential backoff, custom retry conditions, and abort signals."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"handling-temporary-ids","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handling Temporary IDs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When inserting new items into collections where the server generates the final ID, you'll need to handle the transition from temporary to real IDs carefully to avoid UI issues and operation failures."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"the-problem","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Problem"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you insert an item with a temporary ID, the optimistic object is eventually replaced by the synced object with its real server-generated ID. This can cause two issues:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"UI Flicker"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Your UI framework may unmount and remount components when the key changes from temporary to real ID"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Subsequent Operations"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Operations like delete may fail if they try to use the temporary ID before the real ID syncs back"}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Generate temporary ID (e.g., negative number)\nconst tempId = -(Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000000) + 1)\n\n// Insert with temporary ID\ntodoCollection.insert({\n  id: tempId,\n  text: \"New todo\",\n  completed: false\n})\n\n// Problem 1: UI may re-render when tempId is replaced with real ID\n// Problem 2: Trying to delete before sync completes will use tempId\ntodoCollection.delete(tempId) // May 404 on backend","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"solution-1-use-client-generated-uuids","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solution 1: Use Client-Generated UUIDs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your backend supports client-generated IDs, use UUIDs to eliminate the temporary ID problem entirely:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Generate UUID on client\nconst id = crypto.randomUUID()\n\ntodoCollection.insert({\n  id,\n  text: \"New todo\",\n  completed: false\n})\n\n// No flicker - the ID is stable\n// Subsequent operations work immediately\ntodoCollection.delete(id) // Works with the same ID","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the cleanest approach when your backend supports it, as the ID never changes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"solution-2-wait-for-authoritative-sync-or-use-non-optimistic-inserts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solution 2: Wait for Authoritative Sync or Use Non-Optimistic Inserts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure the mutation handler to wait for the server response and the\nauthoritative row to sync before it returns. You can then await handler\nsettlement before enabling subsequent operations. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"when('settled')"},{"type":"text","value":" does\nnot expose or translate the real ID; read it from the synced row or an\napplication-owned response mapping. With a non-optimistic insert, the pending\nitem stays out of the view until the collection publishes synced data."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const handleCreateTodo = async (text: string) => {\n  const tempId = -(Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000000) + 1)\n\n  const tx = todoCollection.insert({\n    id: tempId,\n    text,\n    completed: false\n  })\n\n  // This is an authoritative-sync gate only if onInsert waits for that sync.\n  await tx.when('settled')\n\n  // Do not infer ID readiness from transaction state. Read the synced row or\n  // application-owned mapping, then enable operations with that real ID.\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"solution-3-maintain-a-view-key-mapping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solution 3: Maintain a View Key Mapping"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To avoid UI flicker while keeping optimistic updates, maintain a separate mapping from IDs (both temporary and real) to stable view keys:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Create a mapping API\nconst idToViewKey = new Map<number | string, string>()\n\nfunction getViewKey(id: number | string): string {\n  if (!idToViewKey.has(id)) {\n    idToViewKey.set(id, crypto.randomUUID())\n  }\n  return idToViewKey.get(id)!\n}\n\nfunction linkIds(tempId: number, realId: number) {\n  const viewKey = getViewKey(tempId)\n  idToViewKey.set(realId, viewKey)\n}\n\n// Configure collection to link IDs when real ID comes back\nconst todoCollection = createCollection({\n  id: \"todos\",\n  // ... other options\n  onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    const mutation = transaction.mutations[0]\n    const tempId = mutation.modified.id\n\n    // Create todo on server and get real ID back\n    const response = await api.todos.create({\n      text: mutation.modified.text,\n      completed: mutation.modified.completed,\n    })\n    const realId = response.id\n\n    // Link temp ID to same view key as real ID\n    linkIds(tempId, realId)\n\n    // Wait for sync back\n    await todoCollection.utils.refetch()\n  },\n})\n\n// When inserting with temp ID\nconst tempId = -Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000000) + 1\nconst viewKey = getViewKey(tempId) // Creates and stores mapping\n\ntodoCollection.insert({\n  id: tempId,\n  text: \"New todo\",\n  completed: false\n})\n\n// Use view key for rendering\nconst TodoList = () => {\n  const { data: todos } = useLiveQuery({\n    query: (q) => q.from({ todo: todoCollection }),\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {todos.map((todo) => (\n        <li key={getViewKey(todo.id)}> {/* Stable key */}\n          {todo.text}\n        </li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This pattern maintains a stable key throughout the temporary → real ID transition, preventing your UI framework from unmounting and remounting the component. The view key is stored outside the collection items, so you don't need to add extra fields to your data model."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"best-practices-2","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Best Practices"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use UUIDs when possible"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Client-generated UUIDs eliminate the temporary ID problem"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generate temporary IDs deterministically"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Use negative numbers or a specific pattern to distinguish temporary IDs from real ones"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disable operations on temporary items"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Disable delete/update buttons until persistence completes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Maintain view key mappings"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Create a mapping between IDs and stable view keys for rendering"}]}]}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There's an "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/19","children":[{"type":"text","value":"open issue"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to add better built-in support for temporary ID handling in TanStack DB. This would automate the view key pattern and make it easier to work with server-generated IDs."}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"simplified-mutations-vs-traditional-approaches","text":"Simplified Mutations vs Traditional Approaches","level":3},{"id":"table-of-contents","text":"Table of Contents","level":2},{"id":"mutation-approaches","text":"Mutation Approaches","level":2},{"id":"collection-level-mutations","text":"Collection-Level Mutations","level":3},{"id":"intent-based-mutations-with-custom-actions","text":"Intent-Based Mutations with Custom Actions","level":3},{"id":"bypass-the-mutation-system","text":"Bypass the Mutation System","level":3},{"id":"mutation-lifecycle","text":"Mutation Lifecycle","level":2},{"id":"concurrent-optimistic-transactions","text":"Concurrent Optimistic Transactions","level":3},{"id":"collection-write-operations","text":"Collection Write Operations","level":2},{"id":"insert","text":"Insert","level":3},{"id":"update","text":"Update","level":3},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete","level":3},{"id":"operation-handlers","text":"Operation Handlers","level":2},{"id":"handler-signature","text":"Handler Signature","level":3},{"id":"defining-operation-handlers","text":"Defining Operation Handlers","level":3},{"id":"collection-specific-handler-patterns","text":"Collection-Specific Handler Patterns","level":3},{"id":"generic-mutation-functions","text":"Generic Mutation Functions","level":3},{"id":"schema-validation-in-mutation-handlers","text":"Schema Validation in Mutation Handlers","level":3},{"id":"creating-custom-actions","text":"Creating Custom Actions","level":2},{"id":"basic-action","text":"Basic Action","level":3},{"id":"type-safe-actions-with-schema-validation","text":"Type-Safe Actions with Schema Validation","level":3},{"id":"complex-multi-collection-actions","text":"Complex Multi-Collection Actions","level":3},{"id":"action-parameters","text":"Action Parameters","level":3},{"id":"enforcing-action-boundaries-with-eslint","text":"Enforcing Action Boundaries with ESLint","level":3},{"id":"manual-transactions","text":"Manual Transactions","level":2},{"id":"basic-manual-transaction","text":"Basic Manual Transaction","level":3},{"id":"transaction-configuration","text":"Transaction Configuration","level":3},{"id":"transaction-methods","text":"Transaction Methods","level":3},{"id":"multi-step-workflows","text":"Multi-Step Workflows","level":3},{"id":"using-with-local-collections","text":"Using with Local Collections","level":3},{"id":"why-this-is-needed","text":"Why This Is Needed","level":4},{"id":"basic-usage","text":"Basic Usage","level":4},{"id":"combining-local-and-server-collections","text":"Combining Local and Server Collections","level":4},{"id":"transaction-ordering","text":"Transaction Ordering","level":4},{"id":"best-practices","text":"Best Practices","level":4},{"id":"listening-to-transaction-lifecycle","text":"Listening to Transaction Lifecycle","level":3},{"id":"paced-mutations","text":"Paced Mutations","level":2},{"id":"key-design","text":"Key Design","level":3},{"id":"available-strategies","text":"Available Strategies","level":3},{"id":"debounce-strategy","text":"Debounce Strategy","level":3},{"id":"throttle-strategy","text":"Throttle Strategy","level":3},{"id":"queue-strategy","text":"Queue Strategy","level":3},{"id":"choosing-a-strategy","text":"Choosing a Strategy","level":3},{"id":"using-in-react","text":"Using in React","level":3},{"id":"understanding-queues-and-hook-instances","text":"Understanding Queues and Hook Instances","level":3},{"id":"mutation-merging","text":"Mutation Merging","level":2},{"id":"controlling-optimistic-behavior","text":"Controlling Optimistic Behavior","level":2},{"id":"when-to-disable-optimistic-updates","text":"When to Disable Optimistic Updates","level":3},{"id":"behavior-differences","text":"Behavior Differences","level":3},{"id":"using-non-optimistic-mutations","text":"Using Non-Optimistic Mutations","level":3},{"id":"waiting-for-handler-settlement","text":"Waiting for Handler Settlement","level":3},{"id":"transaction-states","text":"Transaction States","level":2},{"id":"monitoring-transaction-state","text":"Monitoring Transaction State","level":3},{"id":"state-transitions","text":"State Transitions","level":3},{"id":"retry-behavior","text":"Retry Behavior","level":3},{"id":"handling-temporary-ids","text":"Handling Temporary IDs","level":2},{"id":"the-problem","text":"The Problem","level":3},{"id":"solution-1-use-client-generated-uuids","text":"Solution 1: Use Client-Generated UUIDs","level":3},{"id":"solution-2-wait-for-authoritative-sync-or-use-non-optimistic-inserts","text":"Solution 2: Wait for Authoritative Sync or Use Non-Optimistic Inserts","level":3},{"id":"solution-3-maintain-a-view-key-mapping","text":"Solution 3: Maintain a View Key Mapping","level":3},{"id":"best-practices-2","text":"Best Practices","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack DB provides a powerful mutation system that enables optimistic updates with automatic state management. This system is built around a pattern of **optimistic mutation → mutation handler → handler-defined settlement**. A handler can extend that boundary through backend confirmation by waiting for the write to sync back before it returns. This creates a highly responsive user experience while maintaining data consistency and being easy to reason about.\n\nLocal changes are applied immediately as optimistic state. When the mutation handler returns successfully while the transaction is still `persisting`, the transaction completes and the optimistic state is recomputed. Completion proves backend confirmation only when the handler explicitly waited for that confirmation or read-back.\n\nUse [offline transactions](./offline-transactions.md) when a mutation must remain pending across an app restart. Use [SQLite persistence](./sqlite-persistence.md) to retain Collection rows. It can also save sync metadata for supported adapters.\n\n```tsx\n// Define a collection with a mutation handler\nconst todoCollection = createCollection({\n  id: \"todos\",\n  onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    const mutation = transaction.mutations[0]\n    await api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n  },\n})\n\n// Apply an optimistic update\ntodoCollection.update(todo.id, (draft) => {\n  draft.completed = true\n})\n```\n\nThis pattern extends the Redux/Flux unidirectional data flow beyond the client to include the server:\n\n<figure>\n  <a href=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/db/main/docs/unidirectional-data-flow.lg.png\" target=\"_blank\">\n    <img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/db/main/docs/unidirectional-data-flow.png\" />\n  </a>\n</figure>\n\nThe instant inner loop provides optimistic state. The slower outer loop is owned\nby the mutation handler, which can persist to a server and wait for the updated\nserver state to sync back into the collection when that confirmation is\nrequired.\n\n### Simplified Mutations vs Traditional Approaches\n\nTanStack DB's mutation system eliminates much of the boilerplate required for optimistic updates in traditional approaches. Compare the difference:\n\n**Before (TanStack Query with manual optimistic updates):**\n\n```typescript\nconst addTodoMutation = useMutation({\n  mutationFn: async (newTodo) => api.todos.create(newTodo),\n  onMutate: async (newTodo) => {\n    await queryClient.cancelQueries({ queryKey: ['todos'] })\n    const previousTodos = queryClient.getQueryData(['todos'])\n    queryClient.setQueryData(['todos'], (old) => [...(old || []), newTodo])\n    return { previousTodos }\n  },\n  onError: (err, newTodo, context) => {\n    queryClient.setQueryData(['todos'], context.previousTodos)\n  },\n  onSettled: () => {\n    queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['todos'] })\n  },\n})\n```\n\n**After (TanStack DB):**\n\n```typescript\nconst todoCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: ['todos'],\n    queryFn: async () => api.todos.getAll(),\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    schema: todoSchema,\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await Promise.all(\n        transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n          api.todos.create(mutation.modified)\n        )\n      )\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n// Simple mutation - no boilerplate!\ntodoCollection.insert({\n  id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n  text: '🔥 Make app faster',\n  completed: false,\n})\n```\n\nThe benefits:\n- ✅ Automatic optimistic updates\n- ✅ Automatic rollback on error\n- ✅ No manual cache manipulation\n- ✅ Type-safe mutations\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Mutation Approaches](#mutation-approaches)\n- [Mutation Lifecycle](#mutation-lifecycle)\n- [Collection Write Operations](#collection-write-operations)\n- [Operation Handlers](#operation-handlers)\n- [Creating Custom Actions](#creating-custom-actions)\n- [Manual Transactions](#manual-transactions)\n- [Paced Mutations](#paced-mutations)\n- [Mutation Merging](#mutation-merging)\n- [Controlling Optimistic Behavior](#controlling-optimistic-behavior)\n- [Transaction States](#transaction-states)\n- [Handling Temporary IDs](#handling-temporary-ids)\n\n## Mutation Approaches\n\nTanStack DB provides different approaches to mutations, each suited to different use cases:\n\n### Collection-Level Mutations\n\nCollection-level mutations (`insert`, `update`, `delete`) are designed for **direct state manipulation** of a single collection. These are the simplest way to make changes and work well for straightforward CRUD operations.\n\n```tsx\n// Direct state change\ntodoCollection.update(todoId, (draft) => {\n  draft.completed = true\n  draft.completedAt = new Date()\n})\n```\n\nUse collection-level mutations when:\n- You're making simple CRUD operations on a single collection\n- The state changes are straightforward and match what the server will store\n\nYou can use `metadata` to annotate these operations and customize behavior in your handlers:\n\n```tsx\n// Annotate with metadata\ntodoCollection.update(\n  todoId,\n  { metadata: { intent: 'complete' } },\n  (draft) => {\n    draft.completed = true\n  }\n)\n\n// Use metadata in handler\nonUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n  const mutation = transaction.mutations[0]\n\n  if (mutation.metadata?.intent === 'complete') {\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.todos.complete(mutation.original.id)\n      )\n    )\n  } else {\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n      )\n    )\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Intent-Based Mutations with Custom Actions\n\nFor more complex scenarios, use `createOptimisticAction` to create **intent-based mutations** that capture specific user actions.\n\n```tsx\n// Intent: \"like this post\"\nconst likePost = createOptimisticAction<string>({\n  onMutate: (postId) => {\n    // Optimistic guess at the change\n    postCollection.update(postId, (draft) => {\n      draft.likeCount += 1\n      draft.likedByMe = true\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async (postId) => {\n    // Send the intent to the server\n    await api.posts.like(postId)\n    // Server determines actual state changes\n    await postCollection.utils.refetch()\n  },\n})\n\n// Use it.\nlikePost(postId)\n```\n\nUse custom actions when:\n- You need to mutate **multiple collections** in a single transaction\n- The optimistic change is a **guess** at how the server will transform the data\n- You want to send **user intent** to the backend rather than exact state changes\n- The server performs complex logic, calculations, or side effects\n- You want a clean, reusable mutation that captures a specific operation\n\nCustom actions provide the cleanest way to capture specific types of mutations as named operations in your application. While you can achieve similar results using metadata with collection-level mutations, custom actions make the intent explicit and keep related logic together.\n\nTeams that require writes to use actions can [enforce action boundaries with ESLint](#enforcing-action-boundaries-with-eslint).\n\n**When to use each:**\n\n- **Collection-level mutations** (`collection.update`): Simple CRUD operations on a single collection\n- **`createOptimisticAction`**: Intent-based operations, multi-collection mutations, immediately committed\n- **Bypass the mutation system**: Use your existing mutation logic without rewriting\n\n### Bypass the Mutation System\n\nIf you already have mutation logic in an existing system and don't want to rewrite it, you can **completely bypass** TanStack DB's mutation system and use your existing patterns.\n\nWith this approach, you write to the server like normal using your existing logic, then use your collection's mechanism for refetching or syncing data to await the server write. After the sync completes, the collection will have the updated server data and you can render the new state, hide loading indicators, show success messages, navigate to a new page, etc.\n\n```tsx\n// Call your backend directly with your existing logic\nconst handleUpdateTodo = async (todoId, changes) => {\n  await api.todos.update(todoId, changes)\n\n  // Wait for the server change to load into the collection\n  await todoCollection.utils.refetch()\n\n  // Now you know the new data is loaded and can render it or hide loaders\n}\n\n// With Electric\nconst handleUpdateTodo = async (todoId, changes) => {\n  const { txid } = await api.todos.update(todoId, changes)\n\n  // Wait for this specific transaction to sync into the collection\n  await todoCollection.utils.awaitTxId(txid)\n\n  // Now the server change is loaded and you can update UI accordingly\n}\n```\n\nUse this approach when:\n- You have existing mutation logic you don't want to rewrite\n- You're comfortable with your current mutation patterns\n- You want to use TanStack DB only for queries and state management\n\nHow to sync changes back:\n- **QueryCollection**: Refetch with `collection.utils.refetch()` to reload data from the server\n- **ElectricCollection**: Use `collection.utils.awaitTxId(txid)` to wait for a specific transaction to sync\n- **Other sync systems**: Wait for your sync mechanism to update the collection\n\n## Mutation Lifecycle\n\nThe mutation lifecycle follows a consistent pattern across all mutation types:\n\n1. **Optimistic state applied**: The mutation is immediately applied to the local collection as optimistic state\n2. **Handler invoked**: The appropriate handler — either `mutationFn` or a Collection handler (`onInsert`, `onUpdate`, or `onDelete`) — is called to persist the change\n3. **Handler-defined persistence**: Your handler performs the required local or backend work\n4. **Optional confirmation**: If transaction completion must mean server confirmation, the handler waits for the provider's acknowledgement, read-back, or sync observation\n5. **Successful transaction settlement**: If the handler returns while the transaction is still `persisting`, the transaction becomes `completed` and the visible state is recomputed from synced data and any remaining optimistic transactions\n\n```tsx\n// Step 1: Optimistic state applied immediately\ntodoCollection.update(todo.id, (draft) => {\n  draft.completed = true\n})\n// UI updates instantly with optimistic state\n\n// Step 2-3: onUpdate handler performs its persistence work\n// Step 4: The handler may wait for sync back when confirmation is required\n// Step 5: Handler return settles the transaction and recomputes visible state\n```\n\nIf the handler throws an error during persistence, the optimistic state is automatically rolled back.\n\n`tx.when('settled')` observes this transaction-settlement boundary. It resolves\nwith the transaction on success and rejects with the original error on failure\n(or `undefined` for a rollback without an error). It does not by itself prove\nthat a server uploaded, confirmed, or returned the write. It proves those\nstronger guarantees only when the handler waits for that backend observation\nbefore returning. The old `tx.isPersisted.promise` remains available for the\npre-RC releases but is deprecated and will be removed in the 1.0 RC; replace\neach use with `tx.when('settled')`.\n\n### Concurrent Optimistic Transactions\n\nSeparate transactions that update the same row are layered as whole-row\nsnapshots. The latest contributing whole-row snapshot supplies the visible row;\nfields are not independently merged from whichever transactions remain\npending. Each later `update()` draft starts from the current visible state,\nincluding optimistic changes from earlier transactions, so edits can build on\none another.\n\n```tsx\nconst tx1 = docCollection.update(docId, (draft) => {\n  draft.content = 'A'\n})\nconst tx2 = docCollection.update(docId, (draft) => {\n  // This draft sees content = 'A'.\n  draft.content = 'B'\n})\nconst tx3 = docCollection.update(docId, (draft) => {\n  // This draft sees content = 'B'.\n  draft.content = 'C'\n})\n\n// Visible content is now 'C'. If tx1 and then tx2 settle while tx3 is still\n// active, 'C' remains visible.\n```\n\nIf a newer transaction stops contributing while an older one is still active,\nthe older transaction's snapshot can become visible again until that older\ntransaction itself settles or rolls back. Do not infer server execution order\nfrom the local layering order. Multiple writes to the same row inside one\nmanual transaction are different: they merge into that transaction's single\npending mutation according to the\n[mutation-merging rules](#mutation-merging).\n\n## Collection Write Operations\n\nCollections support three core write operations: `insert`, `update`, and `delete`. Each operation applies optimistic state immediately and triggers the corresponding operation handler.\n\n### Insert\n\nAdd new items to a collection:\n\n```typescript\n// Insert a single item\ntodoCollection.insert({\n  id: \"1\",\n  text: \"Buy groceries\",\n  completed: false\n})\n\n// Insert multiple items\ntodoCollection.insert([\n  { id: \"1\", text: \"Buy groceries\", completed: false },\n  { id: \"2\", text: \"Walk dog\", completed: false },\n])\n\n// Insert with metadata\ntodoCollection.insert(\n  { id: \"1\", text: \"Custom item\", completed: false },\n  { metadata: { source: \"import\" } }\n)\n\n// Insert without optimistic updates\ntodoCollection.insert(\n  { id: \"1\", text: \"Server-validated item\", completed: false },\n  { optimistic: false }\n)\n```\n\n**Returns**: A `Transaction` object that you can use to track the mutation's lifecycle.\n\n### Update\n\nModify existing items using an immutable draft pattern:\n\n```typescript\n// Update a single item\ntodoCollection.update(todo.id, (draft) => {\n  draft.completed = true\n})\n\n// Update multiple items\ntodoCollection.update([todo1.id, todo2.id], (drafts) => {\n  drafts.forEach((draft) => {\n    draft.completed = true\n  })\n})\n\n// Update with metadata\ntodoCollection.update(\n  todo.id,\n  { metadata: { reason: \"user update\" } },\n  (draft) => {\n    draft.text = \"Updated text\"\n  }\n)\n\n// Update without optimistic updates\ntodoCollection.update(\n  todo.id,\n  { optimistic: false },\n  (draft) => {\n    draft.status = \"server-validated\"\n  }\n)\n```\n\n**Parameters**:\n- `key` or `keys`: The item key(s) to update\n- `options` (optional): Configuration object with `metadata` and/or `optimistic` flags\n- `updater`: Function that receives a draft to mutate\n\n**Returns**: A `Transaction` object that you can use to track the mutation's lifecycle.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> The `updater` function uses an Immer-like pattern to capture changes as immutable updates. You must not reassign the draft parameter itself—only mutate its properties.\n\nExisting row values stay isolated from draft edits. New objects you assign or\nadd to a draft keep normal shared references during the synchronous callback:\n\n```ts\nconst tag = { label: 'new' }\ntodoCollection.update(todoId, (draft) => {\n  draft.tags.add(tag) // tags is a Set\n  tag.label = 'edited' // included in the update\n  for (const value of draft.tags) value.label = 'final'\n  // tag.label is now 'final' too\n})\ntag.label = 'later' // does not change the stored row\n```\n\nThe completed changes are copied when the callback returns. This applies to\nnew Map values, Set members, and objects assigned to draft properties. If you\nneed to keep a new caller-owned object unchanged during the callback, insert\nyour own copy. A thrown callback does not roll back edits to that caller-owned\nobject; it leaves existing collection data unchanged.\n\nArbitrary class instances are an exception: newly assigned instances stay by\nreference so their methods, prototypes, and private fields remain intact.\nLater changes to such an instance can therefore affect stored data without a\nnew update or notification. Treat those instances as immutable, or convert them\nto plain data before assignment when you need isolation. Supported native values\nsuch as `URL`, `Date`, `RegExp`, ArrayBuffer, DataView, and typed arrays are copied instead. Standard binary views retain their byte range and backing bytes.\n\n### Delete\n\nRemove items from a collection:\n\n```typescript\n// Delete a single item\ntodoCollection.delete(todo.id)\n\n// Delete multiple items\ntodoCollection.delete([todo1.id, todo2.id])\n\n// Delete with metadata\ntodoCollection.delete(todo.id, {\n  metadata: { reason: \"completed\" }\n})\n\n// Delete without optimistic updates\ntodoCollection.delete(todo.id, { optimistic: false })\n```\n\n**Parameters**:\n- `key` or `keys`: The item key(s) to delete\n- `options` (optional): Configuration object with `metadata` and/or `optimistic` flags\n\n**Returns**: A `Transaction` object that you can use to track the mutation's lifecycle.\n\n## Operation Handlers\n\nOperation handlers are functions you provide when creating a collection that handle persisting mutations to your backend. Each collection can define three optional handlers: `onInsert`, `onUpdate`, and `onDelete`.\n\n### Handler Signature\n\nAll operation handlers receive an object with the following properties:\n\n```typescript\ntype OperationHandler = (params: {\n  transaction: Transaction\n  collection: Collection\n}) => Promise<any> | any\n```\n\nThe `transaction` object contains:\n- `mutations`: Array of mutation objects, each with:\n  - `collection`: The collection being mutated\n  - `type`: The mutation type (`'insert'`, `'update'`, or `'delete'`)\n  - `original`: The original item (for updates and deletes)\n  - `modified`: The modified item (for inserts and updates)\n  - `changes`: The changes object (for updates)\n  - `key`: The item key\n  - `metadata`: Optional metadata attached to the mutation\n\n### Defining Operation Handlers\n\nDefine handlers when creating a collection:\n\n```typescript\nconst todoCollection = createCollection({\n  id: \"todos\",\n  // ... other options\n\n  onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.todos.create(mutation.modified)\n      )\n    )\n  },\n\n  onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n      )\n    )\n  },\n\n  onDelete: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.todos.delete(mutation.original.id)\n      )\n    )\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Operation handlers must not resolve until the server changes have synced back to the collection. Different collection types provide different patterns to ensure this happens correctly.\n>\n> Do not call or await `collection.preload()`, live-query `preload()`, or a\n> direct `loadSubset()` inside a mutation handler. The optimistic mutation is\n> already applied when the handler starts. A preload may need a sync commit\n> that is queued behind that same handler, which creates a deadlock. Use the\n> collection adapter's documented mutation acknowledgement pattern instead.\n\n### Collection-Specific Handler Patterns\n\nDifferent collection types have specific patterns for their handlers:\n\n**QueryCollection** - currently refetches automatically after persisting changes. To adopt the explicit v1.0 pattern now:\n```typescript\nonUpdate: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n  await Promise.all(\n    transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n      api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n    )\n  )\n  // Trigger refetch to sync server state\n  await collection.utils.refetch()\n  // Prevent the current pre-1.0 wrapper from refetching a second time.\n  // Remove this return in v1.0.\n  return { refetch: false }\n}\n```\n\n**ElectricCollection** - wait for txid(s) to sync:\n```typescript\nonUpdate: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n  await Promise.all(\n    transaction.mutations.map(async (mutation) => {\n      const response = await api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n      // Wait for this txid to sync\n      await collection.utils.awaitTxId(response.txid)\n    })\n  )\n}\n```\n\n### Generic Mutation Functions\n\nYou can define a single mutation function for your entire app:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { MutationFn } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nconst mutationFn: MutationFn = async ({ transaction }) => {\n  const response = await api.mutations.batch(transaction.mutations)\n\n  if (!response.ok) {\n    throw new Error(`HTTP Error: ${response.status}`)\n  }\n}\n\n// Use in collections\nconst todoCollection = createCollection({\n  id: \"todos\",\n  onInsert: mutationFn,\n  onUpdate: mutationFn,\n  onDelete: mutationFn,\n})\n```\n\n### Schema Validation in Mutation Handlers\n\nWhen a schema is configured for a collection, TanStack DB automatically validates and transforms data during mutations. The mutation handlers receive the **transformed data** (TOutput), not the raw input.\n\n```typescript\nconst todoSchema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  text: z.string(),\n  created_at: z.string().transform(val => new Date(val))  // TInput: string, TOutput: Date\n})\n\nconst collection = createCollection({\n  schema: todoSchema,\n  onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    const item = transaction.mutations[0].modified\n\n    // item.created_at is already a Date object (TOutput)\n    console.log(item.created_at instanceof Date)  // true\n\n    // If your API needs a string, serialize it\n    await api.todos.create({\n      ...item,\n      created_at: item.created_at.toISOString()  // Date → string\n    })\n  }\n})\n\n// User provides string (TInput)\ncollection.insert({\n  id: \"1\",\n  text: \"Task\",\n  created_at: \"2024-01-01T00:00:00Z\"\n})\n```\n\n**Key points:**\n- Schema validation happens **before** mutation handlers are called\n- Handlers receive **TOutput** (transformed data)\n- If your backend needs a different format, serialize in the handler\n- Schema validation errors throw `SchemaValidationError` before handlers run\n\nFor comprehensive documentation on schema validation and transformations, see the [Schemas guide](./schemas.md).\n\n## Creating Custom Actions\n\nFor more complex mutation patterns, use `createOptimisticAction` to create custom actions with full control over the mutation lifecycle.\n\n### Basic Action\n\nCreate an action that combines mutation logic with persistence:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createOptimisticAction } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nconst addTodo = createOptimisticAction<string>({\n  onMutate: (text) => {\n    // Apply optimistic state\n    todoCollection.insert({\n      id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n      text,\n      completed: false,\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async (text, params) => {\n    // Persist to backend\n    const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\", {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      body: JSON.stringify({ text, completed: false }),\n    })\n    const result = await response.json()\n\n    // Wait for sync back\n    await todoCollection.utils.refetch()\n\n    return result\n  },\n})\n\n// Use in components\nconst Todo = () => {\n  const handleClick = () => {\n    addTodo(\"🔥 Make app faster\")\n  }\n\n  return <Button onClick={handleClick} />\n}\n```\n\n### Type-Safe Actions with Schema Validation\n\nFor better type safety and runtime validation, you can use schema validation libraries like Zod, Valibot, or others. Here's an example using Zod:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createOptimisticAction } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { z } from \"zod\"\n\n// Define a schema for the action parameters\nconst addTodoSchema = z.object({\n  text: z.string().min(1, \"Todo text cannot be empty\"),\n  priority: z.enum([\"low\", \"medium\", \"high\"]).optional(),\n})\n\n// Use the schema's inferred type for the generic\nconst addTodo = createOptimisticAction<z.infer<typeof addTodoSchema>>({\n  onMutate: (params) => {\n    // Validate parameters at runtime\n    const validated = addTodoSchema.parse(params)\n\n    // Apply optimistic state\n    todoCollection.insert({\n      id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n      text: validated.text,\n      priority: validated.priority ?? \"medium\",\n      completed: false,\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async (params) => {\n    // Parameters are already validated\n    const validated = addTodoSchema.parse(params)\n\n    const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\", {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      body: JSON.stringify({\n        text: validated.text,\n        priority: validated.priority ?? \"medium\",\n        completed: false,\n      }),\n    })\n    const result = await response.json()\n\n    await todoCollection.utils.refetch()\n    return result\n  },\n})\n\n// Use with type-safe parameters\nconst Todo = () => {\n  const handleClick = () => {\n    addTodo({\n      text: \"🔥 Make app faster\",\n      priority: \"high\",\n    })\n  }\n\n  return <Button onClick={handleClick} />\n}\n```\n\nThis pattern works with any validation library (Zod, Valibot, Yup, etc.) and provides:\n- ✅ Runtime validation of parameters\n- ✅ Type safety from inferred types\n- ✅ Clear error messages for invalid inputs\n- ✅ Single source of truth for parameter shape\n\n### Complex Multi-Collection Actions\n\nActions can mutate multiple collections:\n\n```tsx\nconst createProject = createOptimisticAction<{\n  name: string\n  ownerId: string\n}>({\n  onMutate: ({ name, ownerId }) => {\n    const projectId = crypto.randomUUID()\n\n    // Insert project\n    projectCollection.insert({\n      id: projectId,\n      name,\n      ownerId,\n      createdAt: new Date(),\n    })\n\n    // Update user's project count\n    userCollection.update(ownerId, (draft) => {\n      draft.projectCount += 1\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async ({ name, ownerId }) => {\n    const response = await api.projects.create({ name, ownerId })\n\n    // Wait for both collections to sync\n    await Promise.all([\n      projectCollection.utils.refetch(),\n      userCollection.utils.refetch(),\n    ])\n\n    return response\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Action Parameters\n\nThe `mutationFn` receives additional parameters for advanced use cases:\n\n```tsx\nconst updateTodo = createOptimisticAction<{\n  id: string\n  changes: Partial<Todo>\n}>({\n  onMutate: ({ id, changes }) => {\n    todoCollection.update(id, (draft) => {\n      Object.assign(draft, changes)\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async ({ id, changes }, params) => {\n    // params.transaction contains the transaction object\n    // params.signal is an AbortSignal for cancellation\n\n    const response = await api.todos.update(id, changes, {\n      signal: params.signal,\n    })\n\n    await todoCollection.utils.refetch()\n    return response\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Enforcing Action Boundaries with ESLint\n\nSome teams require UI writes to use actions so shared validation, side effects, and persistence logic remain together. This is an optional application convention. Direct Collection mutations remain appropriate for simple CRUD operations.\n\nThe [React action-enforcement example](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/examples/react/action-enforcement) shows two ESLint policies. Its custom rule permits direct Collection reads in feature code and rejects calls to configured mutation methods on matching Collection imports. The stricter `no-restricted-imports` alternative prohibits those imports, so feature code uses query hooks for reads and actions for writes. See the [example README](https://github.com/TanStack/db/blob/main/examples/react/action-enforcement/README.md#what-is-enforced) for setup, configurable import paths, and the limits of its static analysis.\n\n## Manual Transactions\n\nFor maximum control over transaction lifecycles, create transactions manually using `createTransaction`. This approach allows you to batch multiple mutations, implement custom commit workflows, or create transactions that span multiple user interactions.\n\n### Basic Manual Transaction\n\n```ts\nimport { createTransaction } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nconst addTodoTx = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    // Persist all mutations to backend\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.saveTodo(mutation.modified)\n      )\n    )\n  },\n})\n\n// Apply first change\naddTodoTx.mutate(() =>\n  todoCollection.insert({\n    id: \"1\",\n    text: \"First todo\",\n    completed: false\n  })\n)\n\n// User reviews change...\n\n// Apply another change\naddTodoTx.mutate(() =>\n  todoCollection.insert({\n    id: \"2\",\n    text: \"Second todo\",\n    completed: false\n  })\n)\n\n// User commits when ready (e.g., when they hit save)\naddTodoTx.commit()\n```\n\n### Transaction Configuration\n\nManual transactions accept the following options:\n\n```typescript\ncreateTransaction({\n  id?: string,              // Optional unique identifier for the transaction\n  autoCommit?: boolean,     // Whether to automatically commit after mutate()\n  mutationFn: MutationFn,   // Function to persist mutations\n  metadata?: Record<string, unknown>, // Optional custom metadata\n})\n```\n\n**autoCommit**:\n- `true` (default): Transaction commits immediately after each `mutate()` call\n- `false`: Transaction waits for explicit `commit()` call\n\n### Transaction Methods\n\nManual transactions provide several methods:\n\n```typescript\n// Apply mutations within a transaction\ntx.mutate(() => {\n  collection.insert(item)\n  collection.update(key, updater)\n})\n\n// Commit the transaction\nawait tx.commit()\n\n// Manually rollback changes (e.g., user cancels a form)\n// Note: Rollback happens automatically if mutationFn throws an error\ntx.rollback()\n```\n\nWhen you call collection methods inside `tx.mutate()`, the mutations are\ncaptured by the manual transaction. The collection's `onInsert`, `onUpdate`, and\n`onDelete` handlers are not invoked for those mutations; the\nmanual transaction's `mutationFn` is responsible for persisting\n`transaction.mutations`.\n\nIf a `tx.mutate()` callback throws, that call's optimistic changes are removed\nbefore the error reaches the caller. Earlier successful `tx.mutate()` calls\nremain in the pending transaction. Collection operations can notify subscribers\nwhile the callback runs, so a subscriber may see a change followed by its\nrestoration when a later operation throws.\nIf a subscriber also throws during restoration, `mutate()` throws an\n`AggregateError` whose cause is the original callback error; it still restores\nevery touched Collection.\n\nThis makes manual transactions a good fit for draft-style workflows where local\nstate should update immediately, but persistence should wait for a later user\naction such as Save or Blur. Each `tx.mutate()` call updates the optimistic\nstate instantly, so the UI reflects changes as the user types. When the user\ntriggers Save, `tx.commit()` fires the `mutationFn` to persist all accumulated\nmutations in a single batch. If the user cancels instead, `tx.rollback()`\ndiscards the optimistic changes and reverts the UI.\n\n### Multi-Step Workflows\n\nManual transactions excel at complex workflows:\n\n```ts\nconst reviewTx = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await api.batchUpdate(transaction.mutations)\n  },\n})\n\n// Step 1: User makes initial changes\nreviewTx.mutate(() => {\n  todoCollection.update(id1, (draft) => {\n    draft.status = \"reviewed\"\n  })\n  todoCollection.update(id2, (draft) => {\n    draft.status = \"reviewed\"\n  })\n})\n\n// Step 2: Show preview to user...\n\n// Step 3: User confirms or makes additional changes\nreviewTx.mutate(() => {\n  todoCollection.update(id3, (draft) => {\n    draft.status = \"reviewed\"\n  })\n})\n\n// Step 4: User commits all changes at once\nawait reviewTx.commit()\n// OR user cancels\n// reviewTx.rollback()\n```\n\n### Using with Local Collections\n\nLocalOnly and LocalStorage collections require special handling when used with manual transactions. Unlike server-synced collections that have `onInsert`, `onUpdate`, and `onDelete` handlers automatically invoked, local collections need you to manually accept mutations by calling `utils.acceptMutations()` in your transaction's `mutationFn`.\n\n#### Why This Is Needed\n\nLocal collections (LocalOnly and LocalStorage) don't participate in the standard mutation handler flow for manual transactions. They need an explicit call to persist changes made during `tx.mutate()`.\n\n#### Basic Usage\n\n```ts\nimport { createTransaction } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { localOnlyCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nconst formDraft = createCollection(\n  localOnlyCollectionOptions({\n    id: \"form-draft\",\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  })\n)\n\nconst tx = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    // Make API call with the data first\n    const draftData = transaction.mutations\n      .filter((m) => m.collection === formDraft)\n      .map((m) => m.modified)\n\n    await api.saveDraft(draftData)\n\n    // After API succeeds, accept and persist local collection mutations\n    await formDraft.utils.acceptMutations(transaction)\n  },\n})\n\n// Apply mutations\ntx.mutate(() => {\n  formDraft.insert({ id: \"1\", field: \"value\" })\n})\n\n// Commit when ready\nawait tx.commit()\n```\n\n#### Combining Local and Server Collections\n\nYou can mix local and server collections in the same transaction:\n\n```ts\nconst localSettings = createCollection(\n  localStorageCollectionOptions({\n    id: \"user-settings\",\n    storageKey: \"app-settings\",\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  })\n)\n\nconst userProfile = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"profile\"],\n    queryFn: async () => api.profile.get(),\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await api.profile.update(transaction.mutations[0].modified)\n    },\n  })\n)\n\nconst tx = createTransaction({\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    // Handle server collection mutations explicitly in mutationFn\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations\n        .filter((m) => m.collection === userProfile)\n        .map((m) => api.profile.update(m.modified))\n    )\n\n    // After server mutations succeed, accept local collection mutations\n    await localSettings.utils.acceptMutations(transaction)\n  },\n})\n\n// Update both local and server data in one transaction\ntx.mutate(() => {\n  localSettings.update(\"theme\", (draft) => {\n    draft.mode = \"dark\"\n  })\n  userProfile.update(\"user-1\", (draft) => {\n    draft.name = \"Updated Name\"\n  })\n})\n\nawait tx.commit()\n```\n\n#### Transaction Ordering\n\n**When to call `acceptMutations`** matters for transaction semantics:\n\n**After API success (recommended for consistency):**\n```ts\nmutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n  await api.save(data)  // API call first\n  await localData.utils.acceptMutations(transaction)  // Persist after success\n}\n```\n\n✅ **Pros**: If the API fails, local changes roll back too (all-or-nothing semantics)\n❌ **Cons**: Local state won't reflect changes until API succeeds\n\n**Before API call (for independent local state):**\n```ts\nmutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n  await localData.utils.acceptMutations(transaction)  // Persist first\n  await api.save(data)  // Then API call\n}\n```\n\n✅ **Pros**: Local state persists immediately, regardless of API outcome\n❌ **Cons**: API failure leaves local changes persisted (divergent state)\n\nChoose based on whether your local data should be independent of or coupled to remote mutations.\n\n#### Best Practices\n\n- Always call `utils.acceptMutations()` for local collections in manual transactions. LocalStorage transaction settlement waits for its storage write even if the returned Promise is not awaited; await it when later work depends on the write\n- Call `acceptMutations` **after** API success if you want transactional consistency\n- Call `acceptMutations` **before** API calls if local state should persist regardless\n- Filter mutations by collection if you need to process them separately\n- Mix local and server collections freely in the same transaction\n\n### Listening to Transaction Lifecycle\n\nMonitor transaction state changes:\n\n```typescript\nconst tx = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await api.persist(transaction.mutations)\n  },\n})\n\n// Wait for the transaction handler to settle\ntx.when('settled').then(() => {\n  console.log(\"Transaction completed!\")\n})\n\n// Check current state\nconsole.log(tx.state) // 'pending', 'persisting', 'completed', or 'failed'\n```\n\n## Paced Mutations\n\nPaced mutations provide fine-grained control over **when and how** mutations are persisted to your backend. Instead of persisting every mutation immediately, you can use timing strategies to batch, delay, or queue mutations based on your application's needs.\n\nPowered by [TanStack Pacer](https://github.com/TanStack/pacer), paced mutations are ideal for scenarios like:\n- **Auto-save forms** that wait for the user to stop typing\n- **Slider controls** that need smooth updates without overwhelming the backend\n- **Sequential workflows** where admitted mutations must be attempted in order\n\n### Key Design\n\nThe fundamental difference between strategies is how they handle transactions:\n\n**Debounce/Throttle**: Only one pending transaction (collecting mutations) and one persisting transaction (writing to backend) at a time. Multiple rapid mutations automatically merge together into a single transaction.\n\n**Queue**: Each mutation creates a separate transaction. Admitted mutations run in queue order (FIFO by default, configurable to LIFO). If `maxSize` is set and the waiting queue is full, the returned transaction fails and its optimistic state rolls back.\n\n### Available Strategies\n\n| Strategy | Behavior | Best For |\n|----------|----------|----------|\n| **`debounceStrategy`** | Wait for inactivity before persisting. Only final state is saved. | Auto-save forms, search-as-you-type |\n| **`throttleStrategy`** | Ensure minimum spacing between executions. Mutations between executions are merged. | Sliders, progress updates, analytics |\n| **`queueStrategy`** | Each mutation becomes a separate transaction. Admitted mutations are attempted sequentially in queue order (FIFO by default, configurable to LIFO). | Sequential workflows, file uploads, rate-limited APIs |\n\n### Debounce Strategy\n\nThe debounce strategy waits for a period of inactivity before persisting. This is perfect for auto-save scenarios where you want to wait until the user stops typing before saving their work.\n\n```tsx\nimport { usePacedMutations, debounceStrategy } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nfunction AutoSaveForm({ formId }: { formId: string }) {\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations<{ field: string; value: string }>({\n    onMutate: ({ field, value }) => {\n      // Apply optimistic update immediately\n      formCollection.update(formId, (draft) => {\n        draft[field] = value\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      // Persist the final merged state to the backend\n      await api.forms.save(transaction.mutations)\n    },\n    // Wait 500ms after the last change before persisting\n    strategy: debounceStrategy({ wait: 500 }),\n  })\n\n  const handleChange = (field: string, value: string) => {\n    // Multiple rapid changes merge into a single transaction\n    mutate({ field, value })\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <form>\n      <input onChange={(e) => handleChange('title', e.target.value)} />\n      <textarea onChange={(e) => handleChange('content', e.target.value)} />\n    </form>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n**Key characteristics**:\n- Timer resets on each mutation\n- Only the final merged state persists\n- Reduces backend writes significantly for rapid changes\n- Omitted `trailing` enables trailing execution, even when `leading` is set\n- With `trailing: false`, calls skipped inside the leading window reject with `DebounceCallDroppedError` and roll back their optimistic changes. Both edges disabled reject every call.\n\nCalling `strategy.cleanup()` leaves a pending debounce write scheduled for the quiet edge after its last call. Cleanup returns before the write settles. Await its persistence promise before releasing an external client that the write needs. New debounce calls after cleanup have no defined admission rule.\n\n### Throttle Strategy\n\nThe throttle strategy ensures a minimum spacing between executions. This is ideal for scenarios like sliders or progress updates where you want smooth, consistent updates without overwhelming your backend.\n\n```tsx\nimport { usePacedMutations, throttleStrategy } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nfunction VolumeSlider() {\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations<number>({\n    onMutate: (volume) => {\n      // Apply optimistic update immediately\n      settingsCollection.update('volume', (draft) => {\n        draft.value = volume\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await api.settings.updateVolume(transaction.mutations)\n    },\n    // Persist at most once every 200ms\n    strategy: throttleStrategy({\n      wait: 200,\n      leading: true,   // Execute immediately on first call\n      trailing: true,  // Execute after wait period if there were mutations\n    }),\n  })\n\n  const handleVolumeChange = (volume: number) => {\n    mutate(volume)\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <input\n      type=\"range\"\n      min={0}\n      max={100}\n      onChange={(e) => handleVolumeChange(Number(e.target.value))}\n    />\n  )\n}\n```\n\n**Key characteristics**:\n- Guarantees minimum spacing between persists\n- Can execute on leading edge, trailing edge, or both\n- With `leading: false, trailing: true`, the first write waits until the first trailing edge\n- With `leading: false`, omitting `trailing` also enables the trailing edge\n- With `trailing: false`, calls inside a throttle window fail immediately with `ThrottleCallDroppedError` and roll back their optimistic changes. Omitting `leading` in this case enables the leading edge.\n- With both edges disabled, every call fails with `ThrottleCallDroppedError`\n- Mutations between executions are merged\n\nCalling `strategy.cleanup()` leaves an already scheduled trailing write at its regular edge, so its optimistic transaction and persistence promise can settle. Cleanup returns before that write settles. Stop invoking the mutation function when its strategy is no longer needed; throttle admission after cleanup is not a defined cancellation boundary.\n\n### Queue Strategy\n\nThe queue strategy creates a separate transaction for each mutation and processes admitted transactions sequentially in queue order. Unlike debounce/throttle, **every admitted mutation is attempted**. When `maxSize` is set, overflow is rejected at admission rather than silently dropped.\n\nCalling `strategy.cleanup()` stops new admission. The existing timer drains admitted waiting mutations at the configured `wait` interval and in queue order. Cleanup returns before those writes settle.\nIf the Collection is cleaned up separately, admitted persistence callbacks still run; callers should await the returned transactions when they need those writes to settle before finishing their own teardown.\n\n```tsx\nimport { usePacedMutations, queueStrategy } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nfunction FileUploader() {\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations<File>({\n    onMutate: (file) => {\n      // Apply optimistic update immediately\n      uploadCollection.insert({\n        id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n        file,\n        status: 'pending',\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      // Each file upload is its own transaction\n      const mutation = transaction.mutations[0]\n      await api.files.upload(mutation.modified)\n    },\n    // Process each upload sequentially with 500ms between them\n    strategy: queueStrategy({\n      wait: 500,\n      addItemsTo: 'back',    // FIFO: add to back of queue\n      getItemsFrom: 'front', // FIFO: process from front of queue\n    }),\n  })\n\n  const handleFileSelect = (files: FileList) => {\n    // Each file creates its own transaction, queued for sequential processing\n    Array.from(files).forEach((file) => {\n      mutate(file)\n    })\n  }\n\n  return <input type=\"file\" multiple onChange={(e) => handleFileSelect(e.target.files!)} />\n}\n```\n\n**Key characteristics**:\n- Each mutation becomes its own transaction\n- Processes sequentially in order (FIFO by default)\n- Can configure to LIFO by setting `getItemsFrom: 'back'`\n- Every admitted mutation is attempted (unlike debounce/throttle which may merge intermediate mutations)\n- `maxSize` limits waiting items; overflow fails the returned transaction and rolls back its optimistic state. Admission happens before processing, so `maxSize: 0` rejects even the first mutation.\n- Waits for each transaction to complete before starting the next\n\n**Error handling**:\n- If a mutation fails, **it is not automatically retried** - the transaction transitions to \"failed\" state\n- Failed mutations surface their error via `transaction.when('settled')` (which will reject)\n- Queue overflow rejects `transaction.when('settled')` with `QueueCapacityExceededError`\n- A call after queue cleanup rejects `transaction.when('settled')` with `QueueDisposedError` and rolls back its optimistic mutation\n- **Subsequent mutations continue processing** - a single failure does not block the queue\n- Each mutation is independent; there is no all-or-nothing transaction semantics across multiple mutations\n- To implement retry logic, see [Retry Behavior](#retry-behavior)\n\n### Choosing a Strategy\n\nUse this guide to pick the right strategy for your use case:\n\n**Use `debounceStrategy` when:**\n- You want to wait for the user to finish their action\n- Only the final state matters (intermediate states can be discarded)\n- You want to minimize backend writes\n- Examples: auto-save forms, search-as-you-type, settings panels\n\n**Use `throttleStrategy` when:**\n- You want smooth, consistent updates at a controlled rate\n- Some intermediate states should persist, but not all\n- You need updates to feel responsive without overwhelming the backend\n- Examples: volume sliders, progress bars, analytics tracking, live cursor position\n\n**Use `queueStrategy` when:**\n- Every mutation must persist (no operations can be lost)\n- Order of operations matters\n- You're working with a rate-limited API\n- You need sequential processing with delays\n- Examples: file uploads, batch operations, audit trails, multi-step wizards\n\n### Using in React\n\nThe `usePacedMutations` hook makes it easy to use paced mutations in React components:\n\n```tsx\nimport { usePacedMutations, debounceStrategy } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nfunction MyComponent({ itemId }: { itemId: string }) {\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations<number>({\n    onMutate: (newValue) => {\n      // Apply optimistic update immediately\n      collection.update(itemId, (draft) => {\n        draft.value = newValue\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await api.save(transaction.mutations)\n    },\n    strategy: debounceStrategy({ wait: 500 }),\n  })\n\n  // Each mutate call returns a Transaction you can await\n  const handleSave = async (newValue: number) => {\n    const tx = mutate(newValue)\n\n    // Optionally wait for handler settlement\n    try {\n      await tx.when('settled')\n      console.log('Transaction completed!')\n    } catch (error) {\n      console.error('Save failed:', error)\n    }\n  }\n\n  return <button onClick={() => handleSave(42)}>Save</button>\n}\n```\n\nThe hook automatically memoizes the strategy and mutation function to prevent unnecessary recreations. You can also use `createPacedMutations` directly outside of React:\n\n```ts\nimport { createPacedMutations, queueStrategy } from \"@tanstack/db\"\n\nconst mutate = createPacedMutations<{ id: string; changes: Partial<Item> }>({\n  onMutate: ({ id, changes }) => {\n    // Apply optimistic update immediately\n    collection.update(id, (draft) => {\n      Object.assign(draft, changes)\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await api.save(transaction.mutations)\n  },\n  strategy: queueStrategy({ wait: 200 }),\n})\n\n// Use anywhere in your application\nmutate({ id: '123', changes: { name: 'New Name' } })\n```\n\nA paced mutation's returned transaction is a receipt for strategy-owned\npersistence. Await `tx.when('settled')` to observe completion, or call\n`tx.rollback()` to cancel it. Calling `tx.commit()` throws immediately because\nit would bypass the strategy's timer and serialization. Use `createTransaction`\nfor a workflow that needs manual commit control.\n\nCalls admitted into the same pending paced transaction settle together. If an\n`onMutate` callback calls the paced mutation function again and then throws,\nthe shared transaction rolls back: both the nested call and any earlier call\nmerged into it reject with that error. A later call starts a new transaction.\n\n### Understanding Queues and Hook Instances\n\nEach `usePacedMutations` hook call creates its own transaction group. With a\nfresh strategy object, it also has its own timer or queue.\n\nIf you have multiple components calling `usePacedMutations` separately, each will have its own isolated queue:\n\n```tsx\nfunction EmailDraftEditor1({ draftId }: { draftId: string }) {\n  // This creates Queue A\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations({\n    onMutate: (text) => {\n      draftCollection.update(draftId, (draft) => {\n        draft.text = text\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await api.saveDraft(transaction.mutations)\n    },\n    strategy: debounceStrategy({ wait: 500 }),\n  })\n\n  return <textarea onChange={(e) => mutate(e.target.value)} />\n}\n\nfunction EmailDraftEditor2({ draftId }: { draftId: string }) {\n  // This creates Queue B (separate from Queue A)\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations({\n    onMutate: (text) => {\n      draftCollection.update(draftId, (draft) => {\n        draft.text = text\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await api.saveDraft(transaction.mutations)\n    },\n    strategy: debounceStrategy({ wait: 500 }),\n  })\n\n  return <textarea onChange={(e) => mutate(e.target.value)} />\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, mutations from `EmailDraftEditor1` and `EmailDraftEditor2` will be queued and processed **independently**. They won't share the same debounce timer or queue.\n\n**To share the same queue across multiple components**, create a single `createPacedMutations` instance and use it everywhere:\n\n```tsx\n// Create a single shared instance\nimport { createPacedMutations, debounceStrategy } from \"@tanstack/db\"\n\nexport const mutateDraft = createPacedMutations<{ draftId: string; text: string }>({\n  onMutate: ({ draftId, text }) => {\n    draftCollection.update(draftId, (draft) => {\n      draft.text = text\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await api.saveDraft(transaction.mutations)\n  },\n  strategy: debounceStrategy({ wait: 500 }),\n})\n\n// Now both components share the same queue\nfunction EmailDraftEditor1({ draftId }: { draftId: string }) {\n  return <textarea onChange={(e) => mutateDraft({ draftId, text: e.target.value })} />\n}\n\nfunction EmailDraftEditor2({ draftId }: { draftId: string }) {\n  return <textarea onChange={(e) => mutateDraft({ draftId, text: e.target.value })} />\n}\n```\n\nWith this approach, calls from both components can merge into one pending\ntransaction and share its debounce timer.\n\nTwo separate managers can instead reuse the same strategy object when they\nneed one timing boundary but separate transactions. A shared debounce timer\nwaits for quiet across both managers, then persists each pending transaction.\nA shared throttle spaces their starts, and a shared queue processes their\ntransactions in queue order. Each returned transaction settles independently.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n\n- One manager shared across components lets calls merge into one pending transaction.\n- One strategy object shared across managers gives them one timing boundary while retaining separate transactions.\n- Separate strategy objects give managers independent timers or queues.\n\n## Mutation Merging\n\nWhen multiple mutations operate on the same item within a transaction, TanStack DB intelligently merges them to:\n- **Reduce network traffic**: Fewer mutations sent to the server\n- **Preserve user intent**: Final state matches what user expects\n- **Maintain UI consistency**: Local state always reflects user actions\n\nThe merging behavior follows a truth table based on the mutation types:\n\n| Existing → New      | Result    | Description                                       |\n| ------------------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\n| **insert + update** | `insert`  | Keeps insert type, merges changes, empty original |\n| **insert + delete** | _removed_ | Mutations cancel each other out                   |\n| **update + delete** | `delete`  | Delete dominates                                  |\n| **update + update** | `update`  | Union changes, keep first original                |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Attempting to insert or delete the same item multiple times within a transaction will throw an error.\n\n## Controlling Optimistic Behavior\n\nBy default, all mutations apply optimistic updates immediately to provide instant feedback. You can disable this behavior when you do not want a pending transaction to affect the local view. With `optimistic: false`, the view changes only when the collection receives new synced or local data; successful handler settlement does not publish the mutation by itself.\n\n### When to Disable Optimistic Updates\n\nConsider using `optimistic: false` when:\n\n- **Complex server-side processing**: Operations that depend on server-side generation (e.g., cascading foreign keys, computed fields)\n- **Validation requirements**: Operations where backend validation might reject the change\n- **Confirmation workflows**: Deletes where data should remain visible until confirmed synced data removes it\n- **Batch operations**: Large operations where optimistic rollback would be disruptive\n\n### Behavior Differences\n\n**`optimistic: true` (default)**:\n- Immediately applies mutation to the local store\n- Provides instant UI feedback\n- Requires rollback if server rejects the mutation\n- Best for simple, predictable operations\n\n**`optimistic: false`**:\n- Does not modify the local view while the handler is pending\n- No optimistic view change to roll back\n- The view updates only when the collection publishes new synced or local data\n- Best for complex or validation-heavy operations\n\n### Using Non-Optimistic Mutations\n\n```typescript\n// Critical deletion that needs confirmation\nconst handleDeleteAccount = () => {\n  userCollection.delete(userId, { optimistic: false })\n}\n\n// Server-generated data\nconst handleCreateInvoice = () => {\n  // Server generates invoice number, tax calculations, etc.\n  invoiceCollection.insert(invoiceData, { optimistic: false })\n}\n\n// Mixed approach in same transaction\ntx.mutate(() => {\n  // Instant UI feedback for simple change\n  todoCollection.update(todoId, (draft) => {\n    draft.completed = true\n  })\n\n  // The transaction mutationFn must cause the confirmed data to sync.\n  auditCollection.insert(auditRecord, { optimistic: false })\n})\n```\n\n### Waiting for Handler Settlement\n\nA common pattern with `optimistic: false` is to wait for the mutation handler to\ncomplete before navigating or showing success feedback. If the feedback means\n\"confirmed and published by the server,\" configure the handler to wait for both\nthe confirmation and the relevant sync observation before returning:\n\n```typescript\nconst handleCreatePost = async (postData) => {\n  // Insert without optimistic updates\n  const tx = postsCollection.insert(postData, { optimistic: false })\n\n  try {\n    // Wait for this transaction's handler to complete.\n    await tx.when('settled')\n\n    // This is confirmed and published only if the handler awaited both.\n    navigate(`/posts/${postData.id}`)\n  } catch (error) {\n    // Show error notification\n    toast.error(\"Failed to create post: \" + error.message)\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Transaction States\n\nTransactions progress through the following states during their lifecycle:\n\n1. **`pending`**: Initial state when a transaction is created and optimistic mutations can be applied\n2. **`persisting`**: The transaction's mutation handler is running\n3. **`completed`**: The mutation handler returned successfully while the transaction was still `persisting`\n4. **`failed`**: The transaction was rolled back or its mutation handler threw\n\nThese are local transaction states. `completed` means server-confirmed only if\nthe mutation handler waited for the relevant backend acknowledgement or sync\nobservation before returning.\n\n### Monitoring Transaction State\n\n```typescript\nconst tx = todoCollection.update(todoId, (draft) => {\n  draft.completed = true\n})\n\n// Check current state\nconsole.log(tx.state) // 'pending'\n\n// Wait for specific states\nawait tx.when('settled')\nconsole.log(tx.state) // 'completed'; a rejection takes the transaction to 'failed'\n\n// Handle errors\ntry {\n  await tx.when('settled')\n  console.log(\"Success!\")\n} catch (error) {\n  console.log(\"Failed:\", error)\n}\n```\n\n### State Transitions\n\nThe normal flow is: `pending` → `persisting` → `completed`\n\nIf an error occurs: `pending` → `persisting` → `failed`\n\nFailed transactions automatically rollback their optimistic state.\n\n### Retry Behavior\n\n**Important:** TanStack DB does not automatically retry failed mutations. If a mutation fails (network error, server error, etc.), the transaction transitions to `failed` state and the optimistic state is rolled back. This is by design. Automatic retry logic varies significantly based on your use case (idempotency requirements, error types, backoff strategies, etc.).\n\nTo implement retry logic, wrap your API calls in your `mutationFn`:\n\n```typescript\n// Simple retry helper\nasync function withRetry<T>(\n  fn: () => Promise<T>,\n  maxRetries = 3,\n  delay = 1000\n): Promise<T> {\n  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < maxRetries; attempt++) {\n    try {\n      return await fn()\n    } catch (error) {\n      if (attempt === maxRetries - 1) throw error\n      await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, delay * (attempt + 1)))\n    }\n  }\n  throw new Error('Unreachable')\n}\n\n// Use in your collection\nconst todoCollection = createCollection({\n  id: \"todos\",\n  onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    const mutation = transaction.mutations[0]\n    // Retry up to 3 times with increasing delay\n    await withRetry(() =>\n      api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n    )\n  },\n})\n```\n\nFor more sophisticated retry strategies, consider using a library like [p-retry](https://github.com/sindresorhus/p-retry) which supports exponential backoff, custom retry conditions, and abort signals.\n\n## Handling Temporary IDs\n\nWhen inserting new items into collections where the server generates the final ID, you'll need to handle the transition from temporary to real IDs carefully to avoid UI issues and operation failures.\n\n### The Problem\n\nWhen you insert an item with a temporary ID, the optimistic object is eventually replaced by the synced object with its real server-generated ID. This can cause two issues:\n\n1. **UI Flicker**: Your UI framework may unmount and remount components when the key changes from temporary to real ID\n2. **Subsequent Operations**: Operations like delete may fail if they try to use the temporary ID before the real ID syncs back\n\n```tsx\n// Generate temporary ID (e.g., negative number)\nconst tempId = -(Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000000) + 1)\n\n// Insert with temporary ID\ntodoCollection.insert({\n  id: tempId,\n  text: \"New todo\",\n  completed: false\n})\n\n// Problem 1: UI may re-render when tempId is replaced with real ID\n// Problem 2: Trying to delete before sync completes will use tempId\ntodoCollection.delete(tempId) // May 404 on backend\n```\n\n### Solution 1: Use Client-Generated UUIDs\n\nIf your backend supports client-generated IDs, use UUIDs to eliminate the temporary ID problem entirely:\n\n```tsx\n// Generate UUID on client\nconst id = crypto.randomUUID()\n\ntodoCollection.insert({\n  id,\n  text: \"New todo\",\n  completed: false\n})\n\n// No flicker - the ID is stable\n// Subsequent operations work immediately\ntodoCollection.delete(id) // Works with the same ID\n```\n\nThis is the cleanest approach when your backend supports it, as the ID never changes.\n\n### Solution 2: Wait for Authoritative Sync or Use Non-Optimistic Inserts\n\nConfigure the mutation handler to wait for the server response and the\nauthoritative row to sync before it returns. You can then await handler\nsettlement before enabling subsequent operations. `when('settled')` does\nnot expose or translate the real ID; read it from the synced row or an\napplication-owned response mapping. With a non-optimistic insert, the pending\nitem stays out of the view until the collection publishes synced data.\n\n```tsx\nconst handleCreateTodo = async (text: string) => {\n  const tempId = -(Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000000) + 1)\n\n  const tx = todoCollection.insert({\n    id: tempId,\n    text,\n    completed: false\n  })\n\n  // This is an authoritative-sync gate only if onInsert waits for that sync.\n  await tx.when('settled')\n\n  // Do not infer ID readiness from transaction state. Read the synced row or\n  // application-owned mapping, then enable operations with that real ID.\n}\n```\n\n### Solution 3: Maintain a View Key Mapping\n\nTo avoid UI flicker while keeping optimistic updates, maintain a separate mapping from IDs (both temporary and real) to stable view keys:\n\n```tsx\n// Create a mapping API\nconst idToViewKey = new Map<number | string, string>()\n\nfunction getViewKey(id: number | string): string {\n  if (!idToViewKey.has(id)) {\n    idToViewKey.set(id, crypto.randomUUID())\n  }\n  return idToViewKey.get(id)!\n}\n\nfunction linkIds(tempId: number, realId: number) {\n  const viewKey = getViewKey(tempId)\n  idToViewKey.set(realId, viewKey)\n}\n\n// Configure collection to link IDs when real ID comes back\nconst todoCollection = createCollection({\n  id: \"todos\",\n  // ... other options\n  onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    const mutation = transaction.mutations[0]\n    const tempId = mutation.modified.id\n\n    // Create todo on server and get real ID back\n    const response = await api.todos.create({\n      text: mutation.modified.text,\n      completed: mutation.modified.completed,\n    })\n    const realId = response.id\n\n    // Link temp ID to same view key as real ID\n    linkIds(tempId, realId)\n\n    // Wait for sync back\n    await todoCollection.utils.refetch()\n  },\n})\n\n// When inserting with temp ID\nconst tempId = -Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000000) + 1\nconst viewKey = getViewKey(tempId) // Creates and stores mapping\n\ntodoCollection.insert({\n  id: tempId,\n  text: \"New todo\",\n  completed: false\n})\n\n// Use view key for rendering\nconst TodoList = () => {\n  const { data: todos } = useLiveQuery({\n    query: (q) => q.from({ todo: todoCollection }),\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {todos.map((todo) => (\n        <li key={getViewKey(todo.id)}> {/* Stable key */}\n          {todo.text}\n        </li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThis pattern maintains a stable key throughout the temporary → real ID transition, preventing your UI framework from unmounting and remounting the component. The view key is stored outside the collection items, so you don't need to add extra fields to your data model.\n\n### Best Practices\n\n1. **Use UUIDs when possible**: Client-generated UUIDs eliminate the temporary ID problem\n2. **Generate temporary IDs deterministically**: Use negative numbers or a specific pattern to distinguish temporary IDs from real ones\n3. **Disable operations on temporary items**: Disable delete/update buttons until persistence completes\n4. **Maintain view key mappings**: Create a mapping between IDs and stable view keys for rendering\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> There's an [open issue](https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/19) to add better built-in support for temporary ID handling in TanStack DB. This would automate the view key pattern and make it easier to work with server-generated IDs.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Mutations\nid: mutations\n---\n\nTanStack DB provides a powerful mutation system that enables optimistic updates with automatic state management. This system is built around a pattern of **optimistic mutation → mutation handler → handler-defined settlement**. A handler can extend that boundary through backend confirmation by waiting for the write to sync back before it returns. This creates a highly responsive user experience while maintaining data consistency and being easy to reason about.\n\nLocal changes are applied immediately as optimistic state. When the mutation handler returns successfully while the transaction is still `persisting`, the transaction completes and the optimistic state is recomputed. Completion proves backend confirmation only when the handler explicitly waited for that confirmation or read-back.\n\nUse [offline transactions](./offline-transactions.md) when a mutation must remain pending across an app restart. Use [SQLite persistence](./sqlite-persistence.md) to retain Collection rows. It can also save sync metadata for supported adapters.\n\n```tsx\n// Define a collection with a mutation handler\nconst todoCollection = createCollection({\n  id: \"todos\",\n  onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    const mutation = transaction.mutations[0]\n    await api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n  },\n})\n\n// Apply an optimistic update\ntodoCollection.update(todo.id, (draft) => {\n  draft.completed = true\n})\n```\n\nThis pattern extends the Redux/Flux unidirectional data flow beyond the client to include the server:\n\n<figure>\n  <a href=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/db/main/docs/unidirectional-data-flow.lg.png\" target=\"_blank\">\n    <img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/db/main/docs/unidirectional-data-flow.png\" />\n  </a>\n</figure>\n\nThe instant inner loop provides optimistic state. The slower outer loop is owned\nby the mutation handler, which can persist to a server and wait for the updated\nserver state to sync back into the collection when that confirmation is\nrequired.\n\n### Simplified Mutations vs Traditional Approaches\n\nTanStack DB's mutation system eliminates much of the boilerplate required for optimistic updates in traditional approaches. Compare the difference:\n\n**Before (TanStack Query with manual optimistic updates):**\n\n```typescript\nconst addTodoMutation = useMutation({\n  mutationFn: async (newTodo) => api.todos.create(newTodo),\n  onMutate: async (newTodo) => {\n    await queryClient.cancelQueries({ queryKey: ['todos'] })\n    const previousTodos = queryClient.getQueryData(['todos'])\n    queryClient.setQueryData(['todos'], (old) => [...(old || []), newTodo])\n    return { previousTodos }\n  },\n  onError: (err, newTodo, context) => {\n    queryClient.setQueryData(['todos'], context.previousTodos)\n  },\n  onSettled: () => {\n    queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['todos'] })\n  },\n})\n```\n\n**After (TanStack DB):**\n\n```typescript\nconst todoCollection = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: ['todos'],\n    queryFn: async () => api.todos.getAll(),\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    schema: todoSchema,\n    onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await Promise.all(\n        transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n          api.todos.create(mutation.modified)\n        )\n      )\n    },\n  })\n)\n\n// Simple mutation - no boilerplate!\ntodoCollection.insert({\n  id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n  text: '🔥 Make app faster',\n  completed: false,\n})\n```\n\nThe benefits:\n- ✅ Automatic optimistic updates\n- ✅ Automatic rollback on error\n- ✅ No manual cache manipulation\n- ✅ Type-safe mutations\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n- [Mutation Approaches](#mutation-approaches)\n- [Mutation Lifecycle](#mutation-lifecycle)\n- [Collection Write Operations](#collection-write-operations)\n- [Operation Handlers](#operation-handlers)\n- [Creating Custom Actions](#creating-custom-actions)\n- [Manual Transactions](#manual-transactions)\n- [Paced Mutations](#paced-mutations)\n- [Mutation Merging](#mutation-merging)\n- [Controlling Optimistic Behavior](#controlling-optimistic-behavior)\n- [Transaction States](#transaction-states)\n- [Handling Temporary IDs](#handling-temporary-ids)\n\n## Mutation Approaches\n\nTanStack DB provides different approaches to mutations, each suited to different use cases:\n\n### Collection-Level Mutations\n\nCollection-level mutations (`insert`, `update`, `delete`) are designed for **direct state manipulation** of a single collection. These are the simplest way to make changes and work well for straightforward CRUD operations.\n\n```tsx\n// Direct state change\ntodoCollection.update(todoId, (draft) => {\n  draft.completed = true\n  draft.completedAt = new Date()\n})\n```\n\nUse collection-level mutations when:\n- You're making simple CRUD operations on a single collection\n- The state changes are straightforward and match what the server will store\n\nYou can use `metadata` to annotate these operations and customize behavior in your handlers:\n\n```tsx\n// Annotate with metadata\ntodoCollection.update(\n  todoId,\n  { metadata: { intent: 'complete' } },\n  (draft) => {\n    draft.completed = true\n  }\n)\n\n// Use metadata in handler\nonUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n  const mutation = transaction.mutations[0]\n\n  if (mutation.metadata?.intent === 'complete') {\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.todos.complete(mutation.original.id)\n      )\n    )\n  } else {\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n      )\n    )\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Intent-Based Mutations with Custom Actions\n\nFor more complex scenarios, use `createOptimisticAction` to create **intent-based mutations** that capture specific user actions.\n\n```tsx\n// Intent: \"like this post\"\nconst likePost = createOptimisticAction<string>({\n  onMutate: (postId) => {\n    // Optimistic guess at the change\n    postCollection.update(postId, (draft) => {\n      draft.likeCount += 1\n      draft.likedByMe = true\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async (postId) => {\n    // Send the intent to the server\n    await api.posts.like(postId)\n    // Server determines actual state changes\n    await postCollection.utils.refetch()\n  },\n})\n\n// Use it.\nlikePost(postId)\n```\n\nUse custom actions when:\n- You need to mutate **multiple collections** in a single transaction\n- The optimistic change is a **guess** at how the server will transform the data\n- You want to send **user intent** to the backend rather than exact state changes\n- The server performs complex logic, calculations, or side effects\n- You want a clean, reusable mutation that captures a specific operation\n\nCustom actions provide the cleanest way to capture specific types of mutations as named operations in your application. While you can achieve similar results using metadata with collection-level mutations, custom actions make the intent explicit and keep related logic together.\n\nTeams that require writes to use actions can [enforce action boundaries with ESLint](#enforcing-action-boundaries-with-eslint).\n\n**When to use each:**\n\n- **Collection-level mutations** (`collection.update`): Simple CRUD operations on a single collection\n- **`createOptimisticAction`**: Intent-based operations, multi-collection mutations, immediately committed\n- **Bypass the mutation system**: Use your existing mutation logic without rewriting\n\n### Bypass the Mutation System\n\nIf you already have mutation logic in an existing system and don't want to rewrite it, you can **completely bypass** TanStack DB's mutation system and use your existing patterns.\n\nWith this approach, you write to the server like normal using your existing logic, then use your collection's mechanism for refetching or syncing data to await the server write. After the sync completes, the collection will have the updated server data and you can render the new state, hide loading indicators, show success messages, navigate to a new page, etc.\n\n```tsx\n// Call your backend directly with your existing logic\nconst handleUpdateTodo = async (todoId, changes) => {\n  await api.todos.update(todoId, changes)\n\n  // Wait for the server change to load into the collection\n  await todoCollection.utils.refetch()\n\n  // Now you know the new data is loaded and can render it or hide loaders\n}\n\n// With Electric\nconst handleUpdateTodo = async (todoId, changes) => {\n  const { txid } = await api.todos.update(todoId, changes)\n\n  // Wait for this specific transaction to sync into the collection\n  await todoCollection.utils.awaitTxId(txid)\n\n  // Now the server change is loaded and you can update UI accordingly\n}\n```\n\nUse this approach when:\n- You have existing mutation logic you don't want to rewrite\n- You're comfortable with your current mutation patterns\n- You want to use TanStack DB only for queries and state management\n\nHow to sync changes back:\n- **QueryCollection**: Refetch with `collection.utils.refetch()` to reload data from the server\n- **ElectricCollection**: Use `collection.utils.awaitTxId(txid)` to wait for a specific transaction to sync\n- **Other sync systems**: Wait for your sync mechanism to update the collection\n\n## Mutation Lifecycle\n\nThe mutation lifecycle follows a consistent pattern across all mutation types:\n\n1. **Optimistic state applied**: The mutation is immediately applied to the local collection as optimistic state\n2. **Handler invoked**: The appropriate handler — either `mutationFn` or a Collection handler (`onInsert`, `onUpdate`, or `onDelete`) — is called to persist the change\n3. **Handler-defined persistence**: Your handler performs the required local or backend work\n4. **Optional confirmation**: If transaction completion must mean server confirmation, the handler waits for the provider's acknowledgement, read-back, or sync observation\n5. **Successful transaction settlement**: If the handler returns while the transaction is still `persisting`, the transaction becomes `completed` and the visible state is recomputed from synced data and any remaining optimistic transactions\n\n```tsx\n// Step 1: Optimistic state applied immediately\ntodoCollection.update(todo.id, (draft) => {\n  draft.completed = true\n})\n// UI updates instantly with optimistic state\n\n// Step 2-3: onUpdate handler performs its persistence work\n// Step 4: The handler may wait for sync back when confirmation is required\n// Step 5: Handler return settles the transaction and recomputes visible state\n```\n\nIf the handler throws an error during persistence, the optimistic state is automatically rolled back.\n\n`tx.when('settled')` observes this transaction-settlement boundary. It resolves\nwith the transaction on success and rejects with the original error on failure\n(or `undefined` for a rollback without an error). It does not by itself prove\nthat a server uploaded, confirmed, or returned the write. It proves those\nstronger guarantees only when the handler waits for that backend observation\nbefore returning. The old `tx.isPersisted.promise` remains available for the\npre-RC releases but is deprecated and will be removed in the 1.0 RC; replace\neach use with `tx.when('settled')`.\n\n### Concurrent Optimistic Transactions\n\nSeparate transactions that update the same row are layered as whole-row\nsnapshots. The latest contributing whole-row snapshot supplies the visible row;\nfields are not independently merged from whichever transactions remain\npending. Each later `update()` draft starts from the current visible state,\nincluding optimistic changes from earlier transactions, so edits can build on\none another.\n\n```tsx\nconst tx1 = docCollection.update(docId, (draft) => {\n  draft.content = 'A'\n})\nconst tx2 = docCollection.update(docId, (draft) => {\n  // This draft sees content = 'A'.\n  draft.content = 'B'\n})\nconst tx3 = docCollection.update(docId, (draft) => {\n  // This draft sees content = 'B'.\n  draft.content = 'C'\n})\n\n// Visible content is now 'C'. If tx1 and then tx2 settle while tx3 is still\n// active, 'C' remains visible.\n```\n\nIf a newer transaction stops contributing while an older one is still active,\nthe older transaction's snapshot can become visible again until that older\ntransaction itself settles or rolls back. Do not infer server execution order\nfrom the local layering order. Multiple writes to the same row inside one\nmanual transaction are different: they merge into that transaction's single\npending mutation according to the\n[mutation-merging rules](#mutation-merging).\n\n## Collection Write Operations\n\nCollections support three core write operations: `insert`, `update`, and `delete`. Each operation applies optimistic state immediately and triggers the corresponding operation handler.\n\n### Insert\n\nAdd new items to a collection:\n\n```typescript\n// Insert a single item\ntodoCollection.insert({\n  id: \"1\",\n  text: \"Buy groceries\",\n  completed: false\n})\n\n// Insert multiple items\ntodoCollection.insert([\n  { id: \"1\", text: \"Buy groceries\", completed: false },\n  { id: \"2\", text: \"Walk dog\", completed: false },\n])\n\n// Insert with metadata\ntodoCollection.insert(\n  { id: \"1\", text: \"Custom item\", completed: false },\n  { metadata: { source: \"import\" } }\n)\n\n// Insert without optimistic updates\ntodoCollection.insert(\n  { id: \"1\", text: \"Server-validated item\", completed: false },\n  { optimistic: false }\n)\n```\n\n**Returns**: A `Transaction` object that you can use to track the mutation's lifecycle.\n\n### Update\n\nModify existing items using an immutable draft pattern:\n\n```typescript\n// Update a single item\ntodoCollection.update(todo.id, (draft) => {\n  draft.completed = true\n})\n\n// Update multiple items\ntodoCollection.update([todo1.id, todo2.id], (drafts) => {\n  drafts.forEach((draft) => {\n    draft.completed = true\n  })\n})\n\n// Update with metadata\ntodoCollection.update(\n  todo.id,\n  { metadata: { reason: \"user update\" } },\n  (draft) => {\n    draft.text = \"Updated text\"\n  }\n)\n\n// Update without optimistic updates\ntodoCollection.update(\n  todo.id,\n  { optimistic: false },\n  (draft) => {\n    draft.status = \"server-validated\"\n  }\n)\n```\n\n**Parameters**:\n- `key` or `keys`: The item key(s) to update\n- `options` (optional): Configuration object with `metadata` and/or `optimistic` flags\n- `updater`: Function that receives a draft to mutate\n\n**Returns**: A `Transaction` object that you can use to track the mutation's lifecycle.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> The `updater` function uses an Immer-like pattern to capture changes as immutable updates. You must not reassign the draft parameter itself—only mutate its properties.\n\nExisting row values stay isolated from draft edits. New objects you assign or\nadd to a draft keep normal shared references during the synchronous callback:\n\n```ts\nconst tag = { label: 'new' }\ntodoCollection.update(todoId, (draft) => {\n  draft.tags.add(tag) // tags is a Set\n  tag.label = 'edited' // included in the update\n  for (const value of draft.tags) value.label = 'final'\n  // tag.label is now 'final' too\n})\ntag.label = 'later' // does not change the stored row\n```\n\nThe completed changes are copied when the callback returns. This applies to\nnew Map values, Set members, and objects assigned to draft properties. If you\nneed to keep a new caller-owned object unchanged during the callback, insert\nyour own copy. A thrown callback does not roll back edits to that caller-owned\nobject; it leaves existing collection data unchanged.\n\nArbitrary class instances are an exception: newly assigned instances stay by\nreference so their methods, prototypes, and private fields remain intact.\nLater changes to such an instance can therefore affect stored data without a\nnew update or notification. Treat those instances as immutable, or convert them\nto plain data before assignment when you need isolation. Supported native values\nsuch as `URL`, `Date`, `RegExp`, ArrayBuffer, DataView, and typed arrays are copied instead. Standard binary views retain their byte range and backing bytes.\n\n### Delete\n\nRemove items from a collection:\n\n```typescript\n// Delete a single item\ntodoCollection.delete(todo.id)\n\n// Delete multiple items\ntodoCollection.delete([todo1.id, todo2.id])\n\n// Delete with metadata\ntodoCollection.delete(todo.id, {\n  metadata: { reason: \"completed\" }\n})\n\n// Delete without optimistic updates\ntodoCollection.delete(todo.id, { optimistic: false })\n```\n\n**Parameters**:\n- `key` or `keys`: The item key(s) to delete\n- `options` (optional): Configuration object with `metadata` and/or `optimistic` flags\n\n**Returns**: A `Transaction` object that you can use to track the mutation's lifecycle.\n\n## Operation Handlers\n\nOperation handlers are functions you provide when creating a collection that handle persisting mutations to your backend. Each collection can define three optional handlers: `onInsert`, `onUpdate`, and `onDelete`.\n\n### Handler Signature\n\nAll operation handlers receive an object with the following properties:\n\n```typescript\ntype OperationHandler = (params: {\n  transaction: Transaction\n  collection: Collection\n}) => Promise<any> | any\n```\n\nThe `transaction` object contains:\n- `mutations`: Array of mutation objects, each with:\n  - `collection`: The collection being mutated\n  - `type`: The mutation type (`'insert'`, `'update'`, or `'delete'`)\n  - `original`: The original item (for updates and deletes)\n  - `modified`: The modified item (for inserts and updates)\n  - `changes`: The changes object (for updates)\n  - `key`: The item key\n  - `metadata`: Optional metadata attached to the mutation\n\n### Defining Operation Handlers\n\nDefine handlers when creating a collection:\n\n```typescript\nconst todoCollection = createCollection({\n  id: \"todos\",\n  // ... other options\n\n  onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.todos.create(mutation.modified)\n      )\n    )\n  },\n\n  onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n      )\n    )\n  },\n\n  onDelete: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.todos.delete(mutation.original.id)\n      )\n    )\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Operation handlers must not resolve until the server changes have synced back to the collection. Different collection types provide different patterns to ensure this happens correctly.\n>\n> Do not call or await `collection.preload()`, live-query `preload()`, or a\n> direct `loadSubset()` inside a mutation handler. The optimistic mutation is\n> already applied when the handler starts. A preload may need a sync commit\n> that is queued behind that same handler, which creates a deadlock. Use the\n> collection adapter's documented mutation acknowledgement pattern instead.\n\n### Collection-Specific Handler Patterns\n\nDifferent collection types have specific patterns for their handlers:\n\n**QueryCollection** - currently refetches automatically after persisting changes. To adopt the explicit v1.0 pattern now:\n```typescript\nonUpdate: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n  await Promise.all(\n    transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n      api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n    )\n  )\n  // Trigger refetch to sync server state\n  await collection.utils.refetch()\n  // Prevent the current pre-1.0 wrapper from refetching a second time.\n  // Remove this return in v1.0.\n  return { refetch: false }\n}\n```\n\n**ElectricCollection** - wait for txid(s) to sync:\n```typescript\nonUpdate: async ({ transaction, collection }) => {\n  await Promise.all(\n    transaction.mutations.map(async (mutation) => {\n      const response = await api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n      // Wait for this txid to sync\n      await collection.utils.awaitTxId(response.txid)\n    })\n  )\n}\n```\n\n### Generic Mutation Functions\n\nYou can define a single mutation function for your entire app:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { MutationFn } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nconst mutationFn: MutationFn = async ({ transaction }) => {\n  const response = await api.mutations.batch(transaction.mutations)\n\n  if (!response.ok) {\n    throw new Error(`HTTP Error: ${response.status}`)\n  }\n}\n\n// Use in collections\nconst todoCollection = createCollection({\n  id: \"todos\",\n  onInsert: mutationFn,\n  onUpdate: mutationFn,\n  onDelete: mutationFn,\n})\n```\n\n### Schema Validation in Mutation Handlers\n\nWhen a schema is configured for a collection, TanStack DB automatically validates and transforms data during mutations. The mutation handlers receive the **transformed data** (TOutput), not the raw input.\n\n```typescript\nconst todoSchema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  text: z.string(),\n  created_at: z.string().transform(val => new Date(val))  // TInput: string, TOutput: Date\n})\n\nconst collection = createCollection({\n  schema: todoSchema,\n  onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    const item = transaction.mutations[0].modified\n\n    // item.created_at is already a Date object (TOutput)\n    console.log(item.created_at instanceof Date)  // true\n\n    // If your API needs a string, serialize it\n    await api.todos.create({\n      ...item,\n      created_at: item.created_at.toISOString()  // Date → string\n    })\n  }\n})\n\n// User provides string (TInput)\ncollection.insert({\n  id: \"1\",\n  text: \"Task\",\n  created_at: \"2024-01-01T00:00:00Z\"\n})\n```\n\n**Key points:**\n- Schema validation happens **before** mutation handlers are called\n- Handlers receive **TOutput** (transformed data)\n- If your backend needs a different format, serialize in the handler\n- Schema validation errors throw `SchemaValidationError` before handlers run\n\nFor comprehensive documentation on schema validation and transformations, see the [Schemas guide](./schemas.md).\n\n## Creating Custom Actions\n\nFor more complex mutation patterns, use `createOptimisticAction` to create custom actions with full control over the mutation lifecycle.\n\n### Basic Action\n\nCreate an action that combines mutation logic with persistence:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createOptimisticAction } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nconst addTodo = createOptimisticAction<string>({\n  onMutate: (text) => {\n    // Apply optimistic state\n    todoCollection.insert({\n      id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n      text,\n      completed: false,\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async (text, params) => {\n    // Persist to backend\n    const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\", {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      body: JSON.stringify({ text, completed: false }),\n    })\n    const result = await response.json()\n\n    // Wait for sync back\n    await todoCollection.utils.refetch()\n\n    return result\n  },\n})\n\n// Use in components\nconst Todo = () => {\n  const handleClick = () => {\n    addTodo(\"🔥 Make app faster\")\n  }\n\n  return <Button onClick={handleClick} />\n}\n```\n\n### Type-Safe Actions with Schema Validation\n\nFor better type safety and runtime validation, you can use schema validation libraries like Zod, Valibot, or others. Here's an example using Zod:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createOptimisticAction } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { z } from \"zod\"\n\n// Define a schema for the action parameters\nconst addTodoSchema = z.object({\n  text: z.string().min(1, \"Todo text cannot be empty\"),\n  priority: z.enum([\"low\", \"medium\", \"high\"]).optional(),\n})\n\n// Use the schema's inferred type for the generic\nconst addTodo = createOptimisticAction<z.infer<typeof addTodoSchema>>({\n  onMutate: (params) => {\n    // Validate parameters at runtime\n    const validated = addTodoSchema.parse(params)\n\n    // Apply optimistic state\n    todoCollection.insert({\n      id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n      text: validated.text,\n      priority: validated.priority ?? \"medium\",\n      completed: false,\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async (params) => {\n    // Parameters are already validated\n    const validated = addTodoSchema.parse(params)\n\n    const response = await fetch(\"/api/todos\", {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      body: JSON.stringify({\n        text: validated.text,\n        priority: validated.priority ?? \"medium\",\n        completed: false,\n      }),\n    })\n    const result = await response.json()\n\n    await todoCollection.utils.refetch()\n    return result\n  },\n})\n\n// Use with type-safe parameters\nconst Todo = () => {\n  const handleClick = () => {\n    addTodo({\n      text: \"🔥 Make app faster\",\n      priority: \"high\",\n    })\n  }\n\n  return <Button onClick={handleClick} />\n}\n```\n\nThis pattern works with any validation library (Zod, Valibot, Yup, etc.) and provides:\n- ✅ Runtime validation of parameters\n- ✅ Type safety from inferred types\n- ✅ Clear error messages for invalid inputs\n- ✅ Single source of truth for parameter shape\n\n### Complex Multi-Collection Actions\n\nActions can mutate multiple collections:\n\n```tsx\nconst createProject = createOptimisticAction<{\n  name: string\n  ownerId: string\n}>({\n  onMutate: ({ name, ownerId }) => {\n    const projectId = crypto.randomUUID()\n\n    // Insert project\n    projectCollection.insert({\n      id: projectId,\n      name,\n      ownerId,\n      createdAt: new Date(),\n    })\n\n    // Update user's project count\n    userCollection.update(ownerId, (draft) => {\n      draft.projectCount += 1\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async ({ name, ownerId }) => {\n    const response = await api.projects.create({ name, ownerId })\n\n    // Wait for both collections to sync\n    await Promise.all([\n      projectCollection.utils.refetch(),\n      userCollection.utils.refetch(),\n    ])\n\n    return response\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Action Parameters\n\nThe `mutationFn` receives additional parameters for advanced use cases:\n\n```tsx\nconst updateTodo = createOptimisticAction<{\n  id: string\n  changes: Partial<Todo>\n}>({\n  onMutate: ({ id, changes }) => {\n    todoCollection.update(id, (draft) => {\n      Object.assign(draft, changes)\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async ({ id, changes }, params) => {\n    // params.transaction contains the transaction object\n    // params.signal is an AbortSignal for cancellation\n\n    const response = await api.todos.update(id, changes, {\n      signal: params.signal,\n    })\n\n    await todoCollection.utils.refetch()\n    return response\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Enforcing Action Boundaries with ESLint\n\nSome teams require UI writes to use actions so shared validation, side effects, and persistence logic remain together. This is an optional application convention. Direct Collection mutations remain appropriate for simple CRUD operations.\n\nThe [React action-enforcement example](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/examples/react/action-enforcement) shows two ESLint policies. Its custom rule permits direct Collection reads in feature code and rejects calls to configured mutation methods on matching Collection imports. The stricter `no-restricted-imports` alternative prohibits those imports, so feature code uses query hooks for reads and actions for writes. See the [example README](https://github.com/TanStack/db/blob/main/examples/react/action-enforcement/README.md#what-is-enforced) for setup, configurable import paths, and the limits of its static analysis.\n\n## Manual Transactions\n\nFor maximum control over transaction lifecycles, create transactions manually using `createTransaction`. This approach allows you to batch multiple mutations, implement custom commit workflows, or create transactions that span multiple user interactions.\n\n### Basic Manual Transaction\n\n```ts\nimport { createTransaction } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nconst addTodoTx = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    // Persist all mutations to backend\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations.map((mutation) =>\n        api.saveTodo(mutation.modified)\n      )\n    )\n  },\n})\n\n// Apply first change\naddTodoTx.mutate(() =>\n  todoCollection.insert({\n    id: \"1\",\n    text: \"First todo\",\n    completed: false\n  })\n)\n\n// User reviews change...\n\n// Apply another change\naddTodoTx.mutate(() =>\n  todoCollection.insert({\n    id: \"2\",\n    text: \"Second todo\",\n    completed: false\n  })\n)\n\n// User commits when ready (e.g., when they hit save)\naddTodoTx.commit()\n```\n\n### Transaction Configuration\n\nManual transactions accept the following options:\n\n```typescript\ncreateTransaction({\n  id?: string,              // Optional unique identifier for the transaction\n  autoCommit?: boolean,     // Whether to automatically commit after mutate()\n  mutationFn: MutationFn,   // Function to persist mutations\n  metadata?: Record<string, unknown>, // Optional custom metadata\n})\n```\n\n**autoCommit**:\n- `true` (default): Transaction commits immediately after each `mutate()` call\n- `false`: Transaction waits for explicit `commit()` call\n\n### Transaction Methods\n\nManual transactions provide several methods:\n\n```typescript\n// Apply mutations within a transaction\ntx.mutate(() => {\n  collection.insert(item)\n  collection.update(key, updater)\n})\n\n// Commit the transaction\nawait tx.commit()\n\n// Manually rollback changes (e.g., user cancels a form)\n// Note: Rollback happens automatically if mutationFn throws an error\ntx.rollback()\n```\n\nWhen you call collection methods inside `tx.mutate()`, the mutations are\ncaptured by the manual transaction. The collection's `onInsert`, `onUpdate`, and\n`onDelete` handlers are not invoked for those mutations; the\nmanual transaction's `mutationFn` is responsible for persisting\n`transaction.mutations`.\n\nIf a `tx.mutate()` callback throws, that call's optimistic changes are removed\nbefore the error reaches the caller. Earlier successful `tx.mutate()` calls\nremain in the pending transaction. Collection operations can notify subscribers\nwhile the callback runs, so a subscriber may see a change followed by its\nrestoration when a later operation throws.\nIf a subscriber also throws during restoration, `mutate()` throws an\n`AggregateError` whose cause is the original callback error; it still restores\nevery touched Collection.\n\nThis makes manual transactions a good fit for draft-style workflows where local\nstate should update immediately, but persistence should wait for a later user\naction such as Save or Blur. Each `tx.mutate()` call updates the optimistic\nstate instantly, so the UI reflects changes as the user types. When the user\ntriggers Save, `tx.commit()` fires the `mutationFn` to persist all accumulated\nmutations in a single batch. If the user cancels instead, `tx.rollback()`\ndiscards the optimistic changes and reverts the UI.\n\n### Multi-Step Workflows\n\nManual transactions excel at complex workflows:\n\n```ts\nconst reviewTx = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await api.batchUpdate(transaction.mutations)\n  },\n})\n\n// Step 1: User makes initial changes\nreviewTx.mutate(() => {\n  todoCollection.update(id1, (draft) => {\n    draft.status = \"reviewed\"\n  })\n  todoCollection.update(id2, (draft) => {\n    draft.status = \"reviewed\"\n  })\n})\n\n// Step 2: Show preview to user...\n\n// Step 3: User confirms or makes additional changes\nreviewTx.mutate(() => {\n  todoCollection.update(id3, (draft) => {\n    draft.status = \"reviewed\"\n  })\n})\n\n// Step 4: User commits all changes at once\nawait reviewTx.commit()\n// OR user cancels\n// reviewTx.rollback()\n```\n\n### Using with Local Collections\n\nLocalOnly and LocalStorage collections require special handling when used with manual transactions. Unlike server-synced collections that have `onInsert`, `onUpdate`, and `onDelete` handlers automatically invoked, local collections need you to manually accept mutations by calling `utils.acceptMutations()` in your transaction's `mutationFn`.\n\n#### Why This Is Needed\n\nLocal collections (LocalOnly and LocalStorage) don't participate in the standard mutation handler flow for manual transactions. They need an explicit call to persist changes made during `tx.mutate()`.\n\n#### Basic Usage\n\n```ts\nimport { createTransaction } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\nimport { localOnlyCollectionOptions } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nconst formDraft = createCollection(\n  localOnlyCollectionOptions({\n    id: \"form-draft\",\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  })\n)\n\nconst tx = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    // Make API call with the data first\n    const draftData = transaction.mutations\n      .filter((m) => m.collection === formDraft)\n      .map((m) => m.modified)\n\n    await api.saveDraft(draftData)\n\n    // After API succeeds, accept and persist local collection mutations\n    await formDraft.utils.acceptMutations(transaction)\n  },\n})\n\n// Apply mutations\ntx.mutate(() => {\n  formDraft.insert({ id: \"1\", field: \"value\" })\n})\n\n// Commit when ready\nawait tx.commit()\n```\n\n#### Combining Local and Server Collections\n\nYou can mix local and server collections in the same transaction:\n\n```ts\nconst localSettings = createCollection(\n  localStorageCollectionOptions({\n    id: \"user-settings\",\n    storageKey: \"app-settings\",\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n  })\n)\n\nconst userProfile = createCollection(\n  queryCollectionOptions({\n    queryKey: [\"profile\"],\n    queryFn: async () => api.profile.get(),\n    getKey: (item) => item.id,\n    onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await api.profile.update(transaction.mutations[0].modified)\n    },\n  })\n)\n\nconst tx = createTransaction({\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    // Handle server collection mutations explicitly in mutationFn\n    await Promise.all(\n      transaction.mutations\n        .filter((m) => m.collection === userProfile)\n        .map((m) => api.profile.update(m.modified))\n    )\n\n    // After server mutations succeed, accept local collection mutations\n    await localSettings.utils.acceptMutations(transaction)\n  },\n})\n\n// Update both local and server data in one transaction\ntx.mutate(() => {\n  localSettings.update(\"theme\", (draft) => {\n    draft.mode = \"dark\"\n  })\n  userProfile.update(\"user-1\", (draft) => {\n    draft.name = \"Updated Name\"\n  })\n})\n\nawait tx.commit()\n```\n\n#### Transaction Ordering\n\n**When to call `acceptMutations`** matters for transaction semantics:\n\n**After API success (recommended for consistency):**\n```ts\nmutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n  await api.save(data)  // API call first\n  await localData.utils.acceptMutations(transaction)  // Persist after success\n}\n```\n\n✅ **Pros**: If the API fails, local changes roll back too (all-or-nothing semantics)\n❌ **Cons**: Local state won't reflect changes until API succeeds\n\n**Before API call (for independent local state):**\n```ts\nmutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n  await localData.utils.acceptMutations(transaction)  // Persist first\n  await api.save(data)  // Then API call\n}\n```\n\n✅ **Pros**: Local state persists immediately, regardless of API outcome\n❌ **Cons**: API failure leaves local changes persisted (divergent state)\n\nChoose based on whether your local data should be independent of or coupled to remote mutations.\n\n#### Best Practices\n\n- Always call `utils.acceptMutations()` for local collections in manual transactions. LocalStorage transaction settlement waits for its storage write even if the returned Promise is not awaited; await it when later work depends on the write\n- Call `acceptMutations` **after** API success if you want transactional consistency\n- Call `acceptMutations` **before** API calls if local state should persist regardless\n- Filter mutations by collection if you need to process them separately\n- Mix local and server collections freely in the same transaction\n\n### Listening to Transaction Lifecycle\n\nMonitor transaction state changes:\n\n```typescript\nconst tx = createTransaction({\n  autoCommit: false,\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await api.persist(transaction.mutations)\n  },\n})\n\n// Wait for the transaction handler to settle\ntx.when('settled').then(() => {\n  console.log(\"Transaction completed!\")\n})\n\n// Check current state\nconsole.log(tx.state) // 'pending', 'persisting', 'completed', or 'failed'\n```\n\n## Paced Mutations\n\nPaced mutations provide fine-grained control over **when and how** mutations are persisted to your backend. Instead of persisting every mutation immediately, you can use timing strategies to batch, delay, or queue mutations based on your application's needs.\n\nPowered by [TanStack Pacer](https://github.com/TanStack/pacer), paced mutations are ideal for scenarios like:\n- **Auto-save forms** that wait for the user to stop typing\n- **Slider controls** that need smooth updates without overwhelming the backend\n- **Sequential workflows** where admitted mutations must be attempted in order\n\n### Key Design\n\nThe fundamental difference between strategies is how they handle transactions:\n\n**Debounce/Throttle**: Only one pending transaction (collecting mutations) and one persisting transaction (writing to backend) at a time. Multiple rapid mutations automatically merge together into a single transaction.\n\n**Queue**: Each mutation creates a separate transaction. Admitted mutations run in queue order (FIFO by default, configurable to LIFO). If `maxSize` is set and the waiting queue is full, the returned transaction fails and its optimistic state rolls back.\n\n### Available Strategies\n\n| Strategy | Behavior | Best For |\n|----------|----------|----------|\n| **`debounceStrategy`** | Wait for inactivity before persisting. Only final state is saved. | Auto-save forms, search-as-you-type |\n| **`throttleStrategy`** | Ensure minimum spacing between executions. Mutations between executions are merged. | Sliders, progress updates, analytics |\n| **`queueStrategy`** | Each mutation becomes a separate transaction. Admitted mutations are attempted sequentially in queue order (FIFO by default, configurable to LIFO). | Sequential workflows, file uploads, rate-limited APIs |\n\n### Debounce Strategy\n\nThe debounce strategy waits for a period of inactivity before persisting. This is perfect for auto-save scenarios where you want to wait until the user stops typing before saving their work.\n\n```tsx\nimport { usePacedMutations, debounceStrategy } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nfunction AutoSaveForm({ formId }: { formId: string }) {\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations<{ field: string; value: string }>({\n    onMutate: ({ field, value }) => {\n      // Apply optimistic update immediately\n      formCollection.update(formId, (draft) => {\n        draft[field] = value\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      // Persist the final merged state to the backend\n      await api.forms.save(transaction.mutations)\n    },\n    // Wait 500ms after the last change before persisting\n    strategy: debounceStrategy({ wait: 500 }),\n  })\n\n  const handleChange = (field: string, value: string) => {\n    // Multiple rapid changes merge into a single transaction\n    mutate({ field, value })\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <form>\n      <input onChange={(e) => handleChange('title', e.target.value)} />\n      <textarea onChange={(e) => handleChange('content', e.target.value)} />\n    </form>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n**Key characteristics**:\n- Timer resets on each mutation\n- Only the final merged state persists\n- Reduces backend writes significantly for rapid changes\n- Omitted `trailing` enables trailing execution, even when `leading` is set\n- With `trailing: false`, calls skipped inside the leading window reject with `DebounceCallDroppedError` and roll back their optimistic changes. Both edges disabled reject every call.\n\nCalling `strategy.cleanup()` leaves a pending debounce write scheduled for the quiet edge after its last call. Cleanup returns before the write settles. Await its persistence promise before releasing an external client that the write needs. New debounce calls after cleanup have no defined admission rule.\n\n### Throttle Strategy\n\nThe throttle strategy ensures a minimum spacing between executions. This is ideal for scenarios like sliders or progress updates where you want smooth, consistent updates without overwhelming your backend.\n\n```tsx\nimport { usePacedMutations, throttleStrategy } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nfunction VolumeSlider() {\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations<number>({\n    onMutate: (volume) => {\n      // Apply optimistic update immediately\n      settingsCollection.update('volume', (draft) => {\n        draft.value = volume\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await api.settings.updateVolume(transaction.mutations)\n    },\n    // Persist at most once every 200ms\n    strategy: throttleStrategy({\n      wait: 200,\n      leading: true,   // Execute immediately on first call\n      trailing: true,  // Execute after wait period if there were mutations\n    }),\n  })\n\n  const handleVolumeChange = (volume: number) => {\n    mutate(volume)\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <input\n      type=\"range\"\n      min={0}\n      max={100}\n      onChange={(e) => handleVolumeChange(Number(e.target.value))}\n    />\n  )\n}\n```\n\n**Key characteristics**:\n- Guarantees minimum spacing between persists\n- Can execute on leading edge, trailing edge, or both\n- With `leading: false, trailing: true`, the first write waits until the first trailing edge\n- With `leading: false`, omitting `trailing` also enables the trailing edge\n- With `trailing: false`, calls inside a throttle window fail immediately with `ThrottleCallDroppedError` and roll back their optimistic changes. Omitting `leading` in this case enables the leading edge.\n- With both edges disabled, every call fails with `ThrottleCallDroppedError`\n- Mutations between executions are merged\n\nCalling `strategy.cleanup()` leaves an already scheduled trailing write at its regular edge, so its optimistic transaction and persistence promise can settle. Cleanup returns before that write settles. Stop invoking the mutation function when its strategy is no longer needed; throttle admission after cleanup is not a defined cancellation boundary.\n\n### Queue Strategy\n\nThe queue strategy creates a separate transaction for each mutation and processes admitted transactions sequentially in queue order. Unlike debounce/throttle, **every admitted mutation is attempted**. When `maxSize` is set, overflow is rejected at admission rather than silently dropped.\n\nCalling `strategy.cleanup()` stops new admission. The existing timer drains admitted waiting mutations at the configured `wait` interval and in queue order. Cleanup returns before those writes settle.\nIf the Collection is cleaned up separately, admitted persistence callbacks still run; callers should await the returned transactions when they need those writes to settle before finishing their own teardown.\n\n```tsx\nimport { usePacedMutations, queueStrategy } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nfunction FileUploader() {\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations<File>({\n    onMutate: (file) => {\n      // Apply optimistic update immediately\n      uploadCollection.insert({\n        id: crypto.randomUUID(),\n        file,\n        status: 'pending',\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      // Each file upload is its own transaction\n      const mutation = transaction.mutations[0]\n      await api.files.upload(mutation.modified)\n    },\n    // Process each upload sequentially with 500ms between them\n    strategy: queueStrategy({\n      wait: 500,\n      addItemsTo: 'back',    // FIFO: add to back of queue\n      getItemsFrom: 'front', // FIFO: process from front of queue\n    }),\n  })\n\n  const handleFileSelect = (files: FileList) => {\n    // Each file creates its own transaction, queued for sequential processing\n    Array.from(files).forEach((file) => {\n      mutate(file)\n    })\n  }\n\n  return <input type=\"file\" multiple onChange={(e) => handleFileSelect(e.target.files!)} />\n}\n```\n\n**Key characteristics**:\n- Each mutation becomes its own transaction\n- Processes sequentially in order (FIFO by default)\n- Can configure to LIFO by setting `getItemsFrom: 'back'`\n- Every admitted mutation is attempted (unlike debounce/throttle which may merge intermediate mutations)\n- `maxSize` limits waiting items; overflow fails the returned transaction and rolls back its optimistic state. Admission happens before processing, so `maxSize: 0` rejects even the first mutation.\n- Waits for each transaction to complete before starting the next\n\n**Error handling**:\n- If a mutation fails, **it is not automatically retried** - the transaction transitions to \"failed\" state\n- Failed mutations surface their error via `transaction.when('settled')` (which will reject)\n- Queue overflow rejects `transaction.when('settled')` with `QueueCapacityExceededError`\n- A call after queue cleanup rejects `transaction.when('settled')` with `QueueDisposedError` and rolls back its optimistic mutation\n- **Subsequent mutations continue processing** - a single failure does not block the queue\n- Each mutation is independent; there is no all-or-nothing transaction semantics across multiple mutations\n- To implement retry logic, see [Retry Behavior](#retry-behavior)\n\n### Choosing a Strategy\n\nUse this guide to pick the right strategy for your use case:\n\n**Use `debounceStrategy` when:**\n- You want to wait for the user to finish their action\n- Only the final state matters (intermediate states can be discarded)\n- You want to minimize backend writes\n- Examples: auto-save forms, search-as-you-type, settings panels\n\n**Use `throttleStrategy` when:**\n- You want smooth, consistent updates at a controlled rate\n- Some intermediate states should persist, but not all\n- You need updates to feel responsive without overwhelming the backend\n- Examples: volume sliders, progress bars, analytics tracking, live cursor position\n\n**Use `queueStrategy` when:**\n- Every mutation must persist (no operations can be lost)\n- Order of operations matters\n- You're working with a rate-limited API\n- You need sequential processing with delays\n- Examples: file uploads, batch operations, audit trails, multi-step wizards\n\n### Using in React\n\nThe `usePacedMutations` hook makes it easy to use paced mutations in React components:\n\n```tsx\nimport { usePacedMutations, debounceStrategy } from \"@tanstack/react-db\"\n\nfunction MyComponent({ itemId }: { itemId: string }) {\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations<number>({\n    onMutate: (newValue) => {\n      // Apply optimistic update immediately\n      collection.update(itemId, (draft) => {\n        draft.value = newValue\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await api.save(transaction.mutations)\n    },\n    strategy: debounceStrategy({ wait: 500 }),\n  })\n\n  // Each mutate call returns a Transaction you can await\n  const handleSave = async (newValue: number) => {\n    const tx = mutate(newValue)\n\n    // Optionally wait for handler settlement\n    try {\n      await tx.when('settled')\n      console.log('Transaction completed!')\n    } catch (error) {\n      console.error('Save failed:', error)\n    }\n  }\n\n  return <button onClick={() => handleSave(42)}>Save</button>\n}\n```\n\nThe hook automatically memoizes the strategy and mutation function to prevent unnecessary recreations. You can also use `createPacedMutations` directly outside of React:\n\n```ts\nimport { createPacedMutations, queueStrategy } from \"@tanstack/db\"\n\nconst mutate = createPacedMutations<{ id: string; changes: Partial<Item> }>({\n  onMutate: ({ id, changes }) => {\n    // Apply optimistic update immediately\n    collection.update(id, (draft) => {\n      Object.assign(draft, changes)\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await api.save(transaction.mutations)\n  },\n  strategy: queueStrategy({ wait: 200 }),\n})\n\n// Use anywhere in your application\nmutate({ id: '123', changes: { name: 'New Name' } })\n```\n\nA paced mutation's returned transaction is a receipt for strategy-owned\npersistence. Await `tx.when('settled')` to observe completion, or call\n`tx.rollback()` to cancel it. Calling `tx.commit()` throws immediately because\nit would bypass the strategy's timer and serialization. Use `createTransaction`\nfor a workflow that needs manual commit control.\n\nCalls admitted into the same pending paced transaction settle together. If an\n`onMutate` callback calls the paced mutation function again and then throws,\nthe shared transaction rolls back: both the nested call and any earlier call\nmerged into it reject with that error. A later call starts a new transaction.\n\n### Understanding Queues and Hook Instances\n\nEach `usePacedMutations` hook call creates its own transaction group. With a\nfresh strategy object, it also has its own timer or queue.\n\nIf you have multiple components calling `usePacedMutations` separately, each will have its own isolated queue:\n\n```tsx\nfunction EmailDraftEditor1({ draftId }: { draftId: string }) {\n  // This creates Queue A\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations({\n    onMutate: (text) => {\n      draftCollection.update(draftId, (draft) => {\n        draft.text = text\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await api.saveDraft(transaction.mutations)\n    },\n    strategy: debounceStrategy({ wait: 500 }),\n  })\n\n  return <textarea onChange={(e) => mutate(e.target.value)} />\n}\n\nfunction EmailDraftEditor2({ draftId }: { draftId: string }) {\n  // This creates Queue B (separate from Queue A)\n  const mutate = usePacedMutations({\n    onMutate: (text) => {\n      draftCollection.update(draftId, (draft) => {\n        draft.text = text\n      })\n    },\n    mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n      await api.saveDraft(transaction.mutations)\n    },\n    strategy: debounceStrategy({ wait: 500 }),\n  })\n\n  return <textarea onChange={(e) => mutate(e.target.value)} />\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, mutations from `EmailDraftEditor1` and `EmailDraftEditor2` will be queued and processed **independently**. They won't share the same debounce timer or queue.\n\n**To share the same queue across multiple components**, create a single `createPacedMutations` instance and use it everywhere:\n\n```tsx\n// Create a single shared instance\nimport { createPacedMutations, debounceStrategy } from \"@tanstack/db\"\n\nexport const mutateDraft = createPacedMutations<{ draftId: string; text: string }>({\n  onMutate: ({ draftId, text }) => {\n    draftCollection.update(draftId, (draft) => {\n      draft.text = text\n    })\n  },\n  mutationFn: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    await api.saveDraft(transaction.mutations)\n  },\n  strategy: debounceStrategy({ wait: 500 }),\n})\n\n// Now both components share the same queue\nfunction EmailDraftEditor1({ draftId }: { draftId: string }) {\n  return <textarea onChange={(e) => mutateDraft({ draftId, text: e.target.value })} />\n}\n\nfunction EmailDraftEditor2({ draftId }: { draftId: string }) {\n  return <textarea onChange={(e) => mutateDraft({ draftId, text: e.target.value })} />\n}\n```\n\nWith this approach, calls from both components can merge into one pending\ntransaction and share its debounce timer.\n\nTwo separate managers can instead reuse the same strategy object when they\nneed one timing boundary but separate transactions. A shared debounce timer\nwaits for quiet across both managers, then persists each pending transaction.\nA shared throttle spaces their starts, and a shared queue processes their\ntransactions in queue order. Each returned transaction settles independently.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n\n- One manager shared across components lets calls merge into one pending transaction.\n- One strategy object shared across managers gives them one timing boundary while retaining separate transactions.\n- Separate strategy objects give managers independent timers or queues.\n\n## Mutation Merging\n\nWhen multiple mutations operate on the same item within a transaction, TanStack DB intelligently merges them to:\n- **Reduce network traffic**: Fewer mutations sent to the server\n- **Preserve user intent**: Final state matches what user expects\n- **Maintain UI consistency**: Local state always reflects user actions\n\nThe merging behavior follows a truth table based on the mutation types:\n\n| Existing → New      | Result    | Description                                       |\n| ------------------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\n| **insert + update** | `insert`  | Keeps insert type, merges changes, empty original |\n| **insert + delete** | _removed_ | Mutations cancel each other out                   |\n| **update + delete** | `delete`  | Delete dominates                                  |\n| **update + update** | `update`  | Union changes, keep first original                |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Attempting to insert or delete the same item multiple times within a transaction will throw an error.\n\n## Controlling Optimistic Behavior\n\nBy default, all mutations apply optimistic updates immediately to provide instant feedback. You can disable this behavior when you do not want a pending transaction to affect the local view. With `optimistic: false`, the view changes only when the collection receives new synced or local data; successful handler settlement does not publish the mutation by itself.\n\n### When to Disable Optimistic Updates\n\nConsider using `optimistic: false` when:\n\n- **Complex server-side processing**: Operations that depend on server-side generation (e.g., cascading foreign keys, computed fields)\n- **Validation requirements**: Operations where backend validation might reject the change\n- **Confirmation workflows**: Deletes where data should remain visible until confirmed synced data removes it\n- **Batch operations**: Large operations where optimistic rollback would be disruptive\n\n### Behavior Differences\n\n**`optimistic: true` (default)**:\n- Immediately applies mutation to the local store\n- Provides instant UI feedback\n- Requires rollback if server rejects the mutation\n- Best for simple, predictable operations\n\n**`optimistic: false`**:\n- Does not modify the local view while the handler is pending\n- No optimistic view change to roll back\n- The view updates only when the collection publishes new synced or local data\n- Best for complex or validation-heavy operations\n\n### Using Non-Optimistic Mutations\n\n```typescript\n// Critical deletion that needs confirmation\nconst handleDeleteAccount = () => {\n  userCollection.delete(userId, { optimistic: false })\n}\n\n// Server-generated data\nconst handleCreateInvoice = () => {\n  // Server generates invoice number, tax calculations, etc.\n  invoiceCollection.insert(invoiceData, { optimistic: false })\n}\n\n// Mixed approach in same transaction\ntx.mutate(() => {\n  // Instant UI feedback for simple change\n  todoCollection.update(todoId, (draft) => {\n    draft.completed = true\n  })\n\n  // The transaction mutationFn must cause the confirmed data to sync.\n  auditCollection.insert(auditRecord, { optimistic: false })\n})\n```\n\n### Waiting for Handler Settlement\n\nA common pattern with `optimistic: false` is to wait for the mutation handler to\ncomplete before navigating or showing success feedback. If the feedback means\n\"confirmed and published by the server,\" configure the handler to wait for both\nthe confirmation and the relevant sync observation before returning:\n\n```typescript\nconst handleCreatePost = async (postData) => {\n  // Insert without optimistic updates\n  const tx = postsCollection.insert(postData, { optimistic: false })\n\n  try {\n    // Wait for this transaction's handler to complete.\n    await tx.when('settled')\n\n    // This is confirmed and published only if the handler awaited both.\n    navigate(`/posts/${postData.id}`)\n  } catch (error) {\n    // Show error notification\n    toast.error(\"Failed to create post: \" + error.message)\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Transaction States\n\nTransactions progress through the following states during their lifecycle:\n\n1. **`pending`**: Initial state when a transaction is created and optimistic mutations can be applied\n2. **`persisting`**: The transaction's mutation handler is running\n3. **`completed`**: The mutation handler returned successfully while the transaction was still `persisting`\n4. **`failed`**: The transaction was rolled back or its mutation handler threw\n\nThese are local transaction states. `completed` means server-confirmed only if\nthe mutation handler waited for the relevant backend acknowledgement or sync\nobservation before returning.\n\n### Monitoring Transaction State\n\n```typescript\nconst tx = todoCollection.update(todoId, (draft) => {\n  draft.completed = true\n})\n\n// Check current state\nconsole.log(tx.state) // 'pending'\n\n// Wait for specific states\nawait tx.when('settled')\nconsole.log(tx.state) // 'completed'; a rejection takes the transaction to 'failed'\n\n// Handle errors\ntry {\n  await tx.when('settled')\n  console.log(\"Success!\")\n} catch (error) {\n  console.log(\"Failed:\", error)\n}\n```\n\n### State Transitions\n\nThe normal flow is: `pending` → `persisting` → `completed`\n\nIf an error occurs: `pending` → `persisting` → `failed`\n\nFailed transactions automatically rollback their optimistic state.\n\n### Retry Behavior\n\n**Important:** TanStack DB does not automatically retry failed mutations. If a mutation fails (network error, server error, etc.), the transaction transitions to `failed` state and the optimistic state is rolled back. This is by design. Automatic retry logic varies significantly based on your use case (idempotency requirements, error types, backoff strategies, etc.).\n\nTo implement retry logic, wrap your API calls in your `mutationFn`:\n\n```typescript\n// Simple retry helper\nasync function withRetry<T>(\n  fn: () => Promise<T>,\n  maxRetries = 3,\n  delay = 1000\n): Promise<T> {\n  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < maxRetries; attempt++) {\n    try {\n      return await fn()\n    } catch (error) {\n      if (attempt === maxRetries - 1) throw error\n      await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, delay * (attempt + 1)))\n    }\n  }\n  throw new Error('Unreachable')\n}\n\n// Use in your collection\nconst todoCollection = createCollection({\n  id: \"todos\",\n  onUpdate: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    const mutation = transaction.mutations[0]\n    // Retry up to 3 times with increasing delay\n    await withRetry(() =>\n      api.todos.update(mutation.original.id, mutation.changes)\n    )\n  },\n})\n```\n\nFor more sophisticated retry strategies, consider using a library like [p-retry](https://github.com/sindresorhus/p-retry) which supports exponential backoff, custom retry conditions, and abort signals.\n\n## Handling Temporary IDs\n\nWhen inserting new items into collections where the server generates the final ID, you'll need to handle the transition from temporary to real IDs carefully to avoid UI issues and operation failures.\n\n### The Problem\n\nWhen you insert an item with a temporary ID, the optimistic object is eventually replaced by the synced object with its real server-generated ID. This can cause two issues:\n\n1. **UI Flicker**: Your UI framework may unmount and remount components when the key changes from temporary to real ID\n2. **Subsequent Operations**: Operations like delete may fail if they try to use the temporary ID before the real ID syncs back\n\n```tsx\n// Generate temporary ID (e.g., negative number)\nconst tempId = -(Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000000) + 1)\n\n// Insert with temporary ID\ntodoCollection.insert({\n  id: tempId,\n  text: \"New todo\",\n  completed: false\n})\n\n// Problem 1: UI may re-render when tempId is replaced with real ID\n// Problem 2: Trying to delete before sync completes will use tempId\ntodoCollection.delete(tempId) // May 404 on backend\n```\n\n### Solution 1: Use Client-Generated UUIDs\n\nIf your backend supports client-generated IDs, use UUIDs to eliminate the temporary ID problem entirely:\n\n```tsx\n// Generate UUID on client\nconst id = crypto.randomUUID()\n\ntodoCollection.insert({\n  id,\n  text: \"New todo\",\n  completed: false\n})\n\n// No flicker - the ID is stable\n// Subsequent operations work immediately\ntodoCollection.delete(id) // Works with the same ID\n```\n\nThis is the cleanest approach when your backend supports it, as the ID never changes.\n\n### Solution 2: Wait for Authoritative Sync or Use Non-Optimistic Inserts\n\nConfigure the mutation handler to wait for the server response and the\nauthoritative row to sync before it returns. You can then await handler\nsettlement before enabling subsequent operations. `when('settled')` does\nnot expose or translate the real ID; read it from the synced row or an\napplication-owned response mapping. With a non-optimistic insert, the pending\nitem stays out of the view until the collection publishes synced data.\n\n```tsx\nconst handleCreateTodo = async (text: string) => {\n  const tempId = -(Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000000) + 1)\n\n  const tx = todoCollection.insert({\n    id: tempId,\n    text,\n    completed: false\n  })\n\n  // This is an authoritative-sync gate only if onInsert waits for that sync.\n  await tx.when('settled')\n\n  // Do not infer ID readiness from transaction state. Read the synced row or\n  // application-owned mapping, then enable operations with that real ID.\n}\n```\n\n### Solution 3: Maintain a View Key Mapping\n\nTo avoid UI flicker while keeping optimistic updates, maintain a separate mapping from IDs (both temporary and real) to stable view keys:\n\n```tsx\n// Create a mapping API\nconst idToViewKey = new Map<number | string, string>()\n\nfunction getViewKey(id: number | string): string {\n  if (!idToViewKey.has(id)) {\n    idToViewKey.set(id, crypto.randomUUID())\n  }\n  return idToViewKey.get(id)!\n}\n\nfunction linkIds(tempId: number, realId: number) {\n  const viewKey = getViewKey(tempId)\n  idToViewKey.set(realId, viewKey)\n}\n\n// Configure collection to link IDs when real ID comes back\nconst todoCollection = createCollection({\n  id: \"todos\",\n  // ... other options\n  onInsert: async ({ transaction }) => {\n    const mutation = transaction.mutations[0]\n    const tempId = mutation.modified.id\n\n    // Create todo on server and get real ID back\n    const response = await api.todos.create({\n      text: mutation.modified.text,\n      completed: mutation.modified.completed,\n    })\n    const realId = response.id\n\n    // Link temp ID to same view key as real ID\n    linkIds(tempId, realId)\n\n    // Wait for sync back\n    await todoCollection.utils.refetch()\n  },\n})\n\n// When inserting with temp ID\nconst tempId = -Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000000) + 1\nconst viewKey = getViewKey(tempId) // Creates and stores mapping\n\ntodoCollection.insert({\n  id: tempId,\n  text: \"New todo\",\n  completed: false\n})\n\n// Use view key for rendering\nconst TodoList = () => {\n  const { data: todos } = useLiveQuery({\n    query: (q) => q.from({ todo: todoCollection }),\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {todos.map((todo) => (\n        <li key={getViewKey(todo.id)}> {/* Stable key */}\n          {todo.text}\n        </li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThis pattern maintains a stable key throughout the temporary → real ID transition, preventing your UI framework from unmounting and remounting the component. The view key is stored outside the collection items, so you don't need to add extra fields to your data model.\n\n### Best Practices\n\n1. **Use UUIDs when possible**: Client-generated UUIDs eliminate the temporary ID problem\n2. **Generate temporary IDs deterministically**: Use negative numbers or a specific pattern to distinguish temporary IDs from real ones\n3. **Disable operations on temporary items**: Disable delete/update buttons until persistence completes\n4. **Maintain view key mappings**: Create a mapping between IDs and stable view keys for rendering\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> There's an [open issue](https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/19) to add better built-in support for temporary ID handling in TanStack DB. This would automate the view key pattern and make it easier to work with server-generated IDs.\n"}
